    18	
    19	"She'll float. I never said cross." The ferryman did not take his eyes from the cable, which hung from bank to bank with the brown water almost touching it at the middle. "A week."
    20	
    21	"You said a week on the two hundred and sixty-eighth."
    22	
    23	"It was a week then."
    24	
    25	"That's three days gone out of it."
    26	
    27	"Out of yours. I've my own." He pointed with his chin at a cake of ice the size of a barn door, which was coming down the middle of the channel with a whole young alder frozen into it by the roots. "She hangs on that cable by two blocks. One of those takes a block off me, and I've a boat going down to the mill sideways with whoever's in her. I'll not hang her till the river's done sending them. That's four days of ice yet off the top holds, and after the ice there's the water that was under it." He counted on his thumb. "The two hundred and seventy-seventh, at first light, if she drops in the night. Not before."
