    42	
    43	Seln stood on the landing's last plank with the second hazel wand in his left hand; he drew his thumbnail across it, close under the fortieth notch, and blew the dust away.
    44	
    45	"Forty-one," he said. "A hundred and forty-one days since a clerk's bag went over this water. I have told you what I am counting, and I had better tell you what I expect, since in six days it will stand on that bank whether I have said it or not." He looked across the river at the western landing, where a long stack lay under tarred sheets, and a post with a cord on it, and nobody at all. "There will be a bag. A desk that has been told that a crew kept a surveyor's kit does one of three things. It writes to the crew. It writes to somebody about the crew. Or it writes nothing, and sends."
