    86	
    87	"Which four?" said Oryn.
    88	
    89	It was the first thing that she had asked at that board, and Seln answered her as he would have answered any of them.
    90	
    91	"The four who were enrolled at a house above Ostrand. Not you. And not me; I was never enrolled there. I was employed. What waits for me is older, and it is not kept in a drawer."
    92	
    93	Lira had put her hand into her coat. She brought out a square of stiff paper, folded once, and opened it on the board, where a seal showed at its foot in dull red.
    94	
    95	"Iron, Rank One," she said. "I got it on a plate in Ostrand with half a hall watching. Served how? What does the paper in the drawer do to this one?"
    96	
    97	"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
    98	
    99	"I'd left that on a table," said Brom. "I didn't know that they could hold a thing I never picked up."
