   151	He put it with the rest, as a fact about carts. Seln had turned his head a very little at the words, as he did at a line in a paper that was not quite what it ought to be, and had said nothing, having nothing yet to say. Cael was to remember that afterward: he had lain on a rock for two hours with the whole of it under his eyes, and he had read it as far as the wheels and not a stride further.
   152	
   153	---
   154	
   155	Karis had her leaf of findings on the well's coping, with a stone on it, and the seven of them stood round it in the hold's yard.
   156	
   157	"I shall read you five lines that you have heard, and then I shall say what I think they are worth here," she said. "It hunts what a Path gives off. It hears, and sees a little, and kills on neither. Water masks it. It has no leader, and where nothing moves first, it stands. And it learns." She laid her pencil on the last. "After three had been killed at a ford by people who had declared nothing, the others would not come near such people. I have that from one evening, at one river, and I shall not pretend that it is more. It is all that we have."
   158	
   159	"Three," said Brom. "So we've to kill three, quiet, and the rest pack up."
   160	
   161	"If they are like the others. I think that a hold will not pay us for an *if*."
   162	
   163	"It's what there is," said Cael.
   164	
   165	He had made the plan on the rock, and he gave it to them as he would have laid out a bout, from the ground upward. Brom had gathered a handful of pebbles, without being asked, and he set them down on the coping one at a time as each place was named.
   166	
   167	"They don't come for a cart that keeps its feet. They come for a declaration. So we give them one: small, and short, and a long way from anybody. At the top of the cut, eighty strides up. One point of Ember on a twist of tow, on the ice. It's out in a breath. And whoever sets it doesn't stay there."
   168	
   169	"They go to where it was," said Lira.
   170	
   171	"They go to where it was, and it's gone, and nothing's moving. So they stand. That's her fourth line. Five hounds, or more, standing on a stride of glass at the top of a hill with their heads swinging, and us below them, as quiet as posts." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, twenty strides down from the mark, on the south edge, where there's a flat stone to stand on that isn't ice. Lira above him on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. When they come down to try us, they try Brom first. He's wrapped."
   172	
   173	"I'm always wrapped," said Brom. He had a meal-sack bound round his left forearm already, over the sleeve, with cord. "It's a boot for an arm."
   174	
