   221	The cold came up out of the cut to meet him like the air from a cellar. He kept to the south edge, where a strip of dead turf and gravel ran beside the ice, a little wider than his two boots. The read asked at every stride what the stuff weighed, and Anchor asked *There?* of the wheel-ruts frozen into it, in its thin held note, as it asked at every sill; he told the one that he knew, and the other no. He did not look at the north bank, which was no higher there than his belt, or think of the strip under it that a lip of rock had hidden.
