     1	# Chapter 15 — The Loudest Thing
     2	
     3	He understood it in the time that it took them to cover the first five strides, and it was far too late to be of use.
     4	
     5	They had learned the road. Nothing that had ever declared at the top of that glass had stayed there. It had gone down the hill backward, with a man hanging on its tailboard and a Path blazing in him, and it had come to rest at the foot; and three times the grey things had run to the head of the cut, and found the sound already sixty strides below them. They did not go any longer to where a declaration was made. They went to where it ended.
     6	
     7	And at the foot today there were a small pale woman, and a healer, and a mule.
     8	
     9	He had put them there himself. He had called it the last place in that road that anything would come to.
    10	
    11	They came down the ice as he had never seen a beast go on ice. They did not gallop. They ran low, with their legs spread and their claws out; and where the runnel bent a hand's breadth they let their hindquarters swing wide, and caught themselves, as a boy does on a slide of his own making. They were as high as calves at the shoulder. There were eight of them, strung out over ten strides of glass, and the sound was like a handful of nails drawn down a slate.
    12	
    13	He had come two strides below Brom's stone by then, and he stood there, as he had told six other people to stand. Brom was a post above him, with the rock against his thigh. Something on the scree had gone as still as the scree itself. That was the doctrine, and it was sound. They would go by. They would arrive at the foot, where nothing was loud, and they would stand there and swing their heads. Then one of them would try the nearest quiet body with its teeth.
    14	
    15	Karis knew how to be a post. So did Oryn. He was not certain what a mule knew.
    16	
    17	There was a brown coat at his elbow.
    18	
    19	Vastin had not looked at the hounds since the first of them came over the bank. He was looking down the cut. Cael, who was two feet from him, saw exactly where. A mule's long ears stood against the light, sixty strides off, beside a small figure with something bright in its hand, and a square one with its arms bare. Then the grey head turned, and he looked up the hill at what was coming. His face did not change at all. It was the face with which he had read a case through to its last sheet.
    20	
    21	"I beg your pardon," he said, quite low; and Cael knew afterward that it had been said to him.
    22	
    23	He stepped out into the road.
    24	
    25	It was one stride, bad leg first, off the dead turf and onto the frozen ruts at the runnel's edge. He set his two feet, a little apart, with the stiff leg straight. He took the ash stick in both his wrapped hands and brought its foot down on the road between his boots, once, as a man knocks at a door that he has the right to open.
    26	
    27	There was a sound like a mallet on a cask. It did not come from the stick.
    28	
    29	It came out of the road. Cael felt it through his own soles, since for that one instant the ground under him had been told something and had answered. From the place where the stick stood, a line went across the cut. He saw it run. It went over the frozen ruts and split the runnel's glass from side to side, as straight as a thing ruled, and struck the foot of the north bank. All along that line the ice was white, in a crack no wider than a hair.
    30	
    31	Then the air stood up from it.
