   251	There was an outcrop of the dark plated stone a furlong to the south, as big as a barn, and under its eastern face the ground was green, where a spring came out. It rose in a basin that somebody had built round with stones, a long while ago, and ran over the basin's lip and away down the moor in a thread of rushes. Beside it a single slab of the rock had fallen flat from the face. It lay level, waist-high on its own rubble, twelve feet long and half as broad, with the outcrop standing between it and the wind.
   252	
   253	Karis had stopped in the road.
   254	
   255	She looked at that slab as Cael had seen her look at a wall of cut courses in a marsh. It was off the road by a furlong; a person might speak there. It was in daylight, and dry. And there was not a bench near it, or a meal-sack, or anybody's elbow.
   256	
   257	"That is a table," she said.
   258	
   259	"It's a rock," said Brom.
   260	
   261	"It is the table that I have been looking for since the night before last. I said that I should count in daylight. I have carried six years of an office's paper for two days up a road and through a fight, and I have not yet turned a sheet of it." She had the flat out from under her arm already. "I want the forenoon. I want pebbles, a great many, about the size of a hen's egg, since there is a wind. And I want every person here who can read to wash his hands in that basin first."
   262	
   263	"That knee wants the forenoon as well," said Oryn. "I'd have stopped you at the next water, whether there'd been a table or not."
   264	
   265	Vastin opened his mouth.
   266	
   267	"I am not telling you to sit," she said. "I know better by now. I'm telling you where the mule's stopping."
   268	
   269	Cael looked at the road, which went on westward over the next swell and out of sight. Somewhere beyond that swell there was a fork, and beyond the fork a rise. He had ruled on a job, and the job was done and paid, and nobody had asked him this morning which way they were walking. They had simply walked.
   270	
   271	He went and washed his hands.

