     1	# Chapter 17 — Three Readers
     2	
     3	Karis laid the six rows out along the slab in the order that they had lain in the leather, with the newest at her left hand, and she put a pebble on every corner of every heap before she let go of it.
     4	
     5	The wind could not reach them there. It went over the top of the outcrop with a steady hollow noise, like breath across the mouth of a jar, and now and then a little of it came round the rock's end and lifted a sheet's edge by the width of a finger. The sun stood a hand above the eastern moor. It was a thin white light, with no warmth in it, and it showed every ruled line.
     6	
     7	"I shall count first," she said. "Nobody is to read me anything until I have counted."
     8	
     9	She slipped each tape in turn, and went through the row beneath it by the corners, with her gloved finger and her lips; and she did it twice. She wrote each figure in her notebook under the row's place, and said none of them aloud. It took her the better part of an hour. Nobody hurried her. Lira brought pebbles from the basin's overflow in the lap of her coat, a great many more than were wanted, and set them along the slab's edge in a row, by size.
    10	
    11	Cael had washed his hands with the rest, and he stood at the slab's far side, where every sheet lay the wrong way up to him, as paper always had.
    12	
    13	He had not known where else to stand. He had touched neither the cord nor either strip of brass for a hundred and twelve days, by his own ruling, since a thing that measured had read one way in four hands and another in his. Paper was not a cord. A sheet said what it said, whoever turned it. He told himself so, and kept his hands on the rock's edge.
    14	
    15	Oryn would not come near the slab at all.
    16	
    17	She had unloaded the three boxes herself and turned the mule out on the green. She had looked once at six heaps of an office's paper weighted down with river stones, and her mouth had gone as it went at the smell of a wound that had been left too long. Then she had taken her strip of canvas to the basin, and a cake of grey soap, and begun to wash linen with her back to all of them.
    18	
    19	"That's your trade," she said, when Lira asked. "I'll hear what it comes to. I'll not stand and watch it done."
    20	
    21	Brom sat on the basin's rim with his right boot off and his bound toes in the overflow, which was as cold as a knife. He had been told by nobody to do it, and the healer had looked at the foot, and said that it was sense, and gone on wringing.
    22	
    23	"Now," said Karis.
    24	
    25	She had gone back to the uppermost row. She went through it a third time, quickly, looking only at the heads, and she laid a finger of her left glove on the rock for every group of four signs that she had not yet met in that row. The thumb went down last. She did the same with the second row, and the third, and so to the sixth, and each time the thumb went down, and no sixth finger was wanted.
    26	
    27	"Five," she said. "I said more than four, by a lamp, and that I should give a number when I had checked it in daylight. It is five. It is the same five in every one of six years; I cannot read a sign of them, and I can tell one group from another as I can tell your five faces."
    28	
    29	"Four on the hide," said Brom. "And one over."
    30	
    31	"Wait. I do not yet know that our four are among them. I have only supposed it." She had the calfskin out, and she unrolled it at the slab's end, under four pebbles of her own choosing, with the pencil line running through its four rings. "The Stair I know, since his book gives me its signs. For the others I have only the measures. Three of our places were of one size, and the fourth was twice that, by the bend of its fence. If these sheets say the same, I shall believe them."
    32	
    33	She took one sheet from each of four groups, and laid them side by side, and read down the third column of each with her lips moving. A cord knotted at the fives had been run out from a centre to an edge. She did not need to turn lengths and knots into anything; she needed only to see whether four entries agreed.
    34	
    35	"Three alike, to the stride," she said. "And one that is double. They are ours."
    36	
    37	"And the fifth?" said Lira.
    38	
    39	Karis looked at the fifth group's sheet, which she had not lifted from its row.
    40	
    41	"The fifth is larger again than the fourth. I have read its measure and nothing else, and I shall say nothing else until I have turned every sheet that it has. It is not on the hide. I do not know where it is. These are an office's signs for a place, and I cannot read them; if I could, I should not have needed to walk here."
    42	
    43	Nobody spoke. Cael looked at the calfskin, where a ruled line went through four rings and off the edge of the skin onto bare rock. He had watched a small pale woman slide a strip of brass along that line, a hand at a time, on the first night of a cold term, past the fourth ring and onto the table, as though the deal might have something to say about where it went next.
    44	
    45	---
    46	
    47	"Then I shall read for what repeats," said Karis. "It is the only reading that I am good for, with a text whose words are shut. Seln, you will read for what is not there; you have told me a dozen times that it is the half of any paper. And you—" she had turned to the man who stood at the slab's end on his stick, with his bound leg a little out— "will tell me what the form is. I do not know this office. I do not know what a clerk in it is obliged to write, and so I cannot tell what he has chosen to."
    48	
    49	"I can tell you that," said Vastin. "It is very nearly the only thing that I am still able to do."
    50	
    51	He had not sat. Oryn had rebound the knee at the basin as soon as they halted, with him standing against the rock, and had said nothing to him about stones.
    52	
    53	Karis put one gloved finger at the head of the nearest sheet.
    54	
    55	"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the office's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."
    56	
    57	She went down them, a column at a time, across all six rows, and it took her a long while, and she gave it to them as she found it.
    58	
    59	The measure did not change. That was the first thing, and she read it three times before she would say it. Each of the five places had been run out from its eight stations in every one of six years, by four different surveyors, to judge by the figures' shapes; and not one radius had altered by a single stride. She had expected that a thing would grow, or shrink. A marsh did, and a river-bed. These had sat where they were as a millstone sits.
    60	
    61	"Whatever they are, they are not spreading," she said. "And they are not going away. I had not known until this morning that I was afraid of the one or hoping for the other."
    62	
    63	The fifth column she had read by a lamp already. There were three clerks' hands in six years: a round one, and a cramped one that leaned backward, and one that made its capitals like gates. Each of them had written the same single word against every station of every place.
    64	
    65	"The fourth is the one that I had not looked at," said Karis.
    66	
    67	She was silent over it for some time.
    68	
    69	"He is required to say how his instruments behaved. I suppose that somebody once wondered whether a cord, or a level, would go wrong in such a place as a sigil does. So there is a column. And in it, against every station, for six years, in four hands, there is one entry." She read it out. "*Cord true. Edge true. Level true.*"
    70	
    71	Cael felt it in his stomach before he had understood why.
    72	
    73	Nobody looked at him. He was grateful for that, and he knew what it cost at least one of them. A surveyor's cord had been carried into each of those places, year upon year, by men whose business was to notice if it read amiss; and it had read true, every time, as it had in an inn yard for Karis and Lira and Brom and Seln. There had never been anything the matter with the cord.
    74	
    75	Karis did not lift her eyes from the sheet. She had a hair pressed in the fold of her notebook, and a line under it that said *no inference entered*, and she turned the leaf of the abstract over a little faster than she had turned the others.
    76	
    77	"There is a sixth thing on the older forms," she said. "It is not a column. It is a space at the foot, ruled off, and headed *Remarks*. It is empty on every sheet in the three lowest rows but one."
    78	
    79	She drew that one out. It was from the fourth row, and it had the Stair's signs at its head, and the number thirty-two. At its foot, in the ruled space, there were four words in a hand that was small, and upright, and pressed hard.
    80	
    81	She did not read them at once. She reached behind her, to the tarred case, and brought out the dead man's ledger, and laid it open beside the sheet; and she looked from the one to the other for as long as it took Brom to draw his foot out of the water.
    82	
    83	"It is his," she said. "I should not swear to a hand on four words. I shall say that I see no difference." She read it then, to the whole slab, in the voice that she kept for another person's finding.
    84	
    85	*Behaves as device. Cannot be.*
    86	
    87	"What does?" said Brom.
    88	
    89	"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write." Her finger lay beside the ruled space, not on it. "He was a man who lived among instruments. He knew how a made thing behaves: it does the same at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule. That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."
    90	
    91	"Why can't it?"
    92	
    93	"He does not say. I suppose that nobody could tell him who had made such a thing, or how it would be made; and a surveyor is not paid to suppose." She had taken her hand away. "I felt it, at three of those lines. So did four of you. There was no dimming. It went out at one stride, for a third rank and a first and a Copper alike, and came back at the same stride, whole. It was not as a person tires. It was as a row of lamps—"
    94	
    95	She stopped.
    96	
    97	Cael had never seen her stop so. Her mouth stayed a little open on the next word, and he watched her look at it, as she would have looked at a figure that had come out too neatly.
    98	
    99	"No," said Karis. "I have a rule for this, and I said it twice in a yard. I was about to tell you what a thing is from how it felt in my own hand, and my hand is the only ruler that I had there. I shall not finish that."
   100	
   101	"You'd got as far as lamps," said Lira.
   102	
   103	"I had got a good deal too far."
   104	
   105	---
   106	
   107	Seln had read all six rows while she was at the fourth column, standing at her shoulder and then at the next heap, and he had not touched a pebble that he did not put back.
   108	
   109	"I have five things that are not there," he said. "I shall give them in the order of their weight, which is the reverse of the order in which a person notices them."
   110	
   111	He had his hands in his sleeves. He spoke to the paper.
   112	
   113	"There is no routing head on any sheet. That you knew. There is no surveyor's name; a field book is signed, and an abstract ought to carry the name that the book carried, and these carry a number in its place. There is nothing to say who ordered a man to walk round the same five places for six years. And there is no column for a cause." He let that stand a moment. "A form is a list of the questions that its maker expected to be asked. Somebody expected to be asked the day, and the measure, and whether the cord was true. Nobody expected to be asked why the thing happens. I have never before seen an office measure a matter so carefully and show so little wish to understand it."
   114	
   115	"That is four," said Karis.
   116	
   117	"The fifth is yours. You found the remark; I looked for what came after it." He drew a sheet from the third row, and one from the second, and laid them beside the Stair's. "On the form that he wrote upon, there is a space at the foot for remarks. On the next year's printing there is none. The rule that closed it off has been taken out of the block, and the fifth column runs to the foot of the sheet. Somebody read those four words. It is the only sign in six rows that anybody read anything at all; and what he did, having read them, was to see that no surveyor should have room to write four more."
   118	
   119	Lira let her breath go through her teeth.
   120	
   121	"And the man who wrote them went down forty steps with a stake," said Brom slowly, "and never came up."
   122	
   123	"I do not join those," said Seln. "I only lay them where they can be seen."
   124	
   125	Vastin had listened to the whole of it with his head a little bent, as he might have listened to a junior presenting a file. When he spoke it was to Karis, since it was she who had asked him.
   126	
   127	"You wished to know what the form is. It is a survey abstract of the second schedule: that is to say, it is the sheet that stands in place of the field book when the book has been called in. The book itself does not survive it. I think that you hold the only one that ever escaped; and it escaped because its owner did not come home." His left hand, which would not open, lay on the stick's head. "The sheets are drawn at the year's end, in the office that has the charge of the stations. You will have seen that the days run strangely."
   128	
   129	"I have seen that I cannot make them agree with the broker's paper, which gives a day past the Line."
   130	
   131	"You will not. That office does not keep the seat's year; it keeps its own, which opens later. It is an old quarrel about who was founded first, and I shall not weary you with it. A day that is written in the stations' count must be turned before it can be laid beside a registry date, and the turning is a matter of taking off the difference." He gave the difference, which was a number of days that any child could have subtracted, and Karis wrote it in her margin. "Every junior does that sum in his first week, and gets it wrong once. A return that comes up from a station is dated so. A paper that goes down from the seat, to a district or to a person, is dated the other way."
   132	
   133	"Thank you," said Karis. "That is the first thing in these rows that anybody has been able to explain to me."
   134	
   135	He had been looking along the slab while he spoke: at six loose heaps, and their tapes lying in a tangle, and the wind lifting a corner.
   136	
   137	"It should be directed that each row be taken up singly, and re-taped before the next is opened," he said, in a different voice, level and a little quick. "Under two hands. A schedule to be made of every sheet as it is turned, with its row and its place in the row, and the schedule to be signed by—"
   138	
   139	He stopped. Nobody had interrupted him. Karis had her pencil lifted, and was regarding him over it with her head on one side; and Seln, at the next heap, had turned his eyes from the paper to the man, and kept them there, and said nothing whatever.
   140	
   141	"I beg your pardon. I was drafting."
   142	
   143	"It is quite a good order," said Karis. "I have done the first half of it already. I have nobody to sign the second."
   144	
   145	"No." He looked at his own hand on the stick. "No. Of course."
   146	
   147	It was not a quarter of an hour before he did the other thing. She had laid the Stair's sheet back, with the four words at its foot, and he had bent to read them for himself; and Cael, who was watching, saw the grey head come up very sharply.
   148	
   149	"That is restricted matter. A finding of that kind, upon an instrument of that kind, would stand under the first article of the closed schedule, and no person below a seat ought to have sight of it without—" The quick level voice ran on for three words more, and died. "There is no schedule here. I find that I had classed it before I had finished reading it."
   150	
   151	"What class were we?" said Lira.
   152	
   153	"The class of persons to whom it may not be shown."
   154	
   155	"That's been most of my life," said Lira. "It's a comfort to hear it from the man who kept the list."
   156	
   157	He took that without a word. Seln's eyes had not left him.
   158	
   159	---
   160	
   161	"There is a thing at the head of each sheet which I have left until last," said Karis, "since I knew that I should not be able to keep my temper with it."
   162	
   163	She had the four sheets side by side again, the three that were alike and the one that was double. She laid her pencil along the top of them. To the right of the four signs and the number, on every one, a small square had been ruled off by the printer, with a heading above it in the same signs that nobody could read; and in the square, in ink, a clerk had made a mark.
   164	
   165	It was a short stroke, and beneath the stroke a bar.
   166	
   167	Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of an iron stake that lay in a tarred case, three feet from her elbow, with its point still bright. It was on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.
   168	
   169	"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."
   170	
   171	"So it's nothing to do with which got fenced," said Brom.
   172	
   173	"No. And it is nothing to do with size." She laid her finger on the sheet whose measure was double. "This place is twice the others, and it has the same stroke and the same bar as they have, drawn no larger and no differently. Whatever that square is for, it is not for how large a thing is."
   174	
   175	"Then what's it for?" said Lira.
   176	
   177	"It has a square. A printer cut a block with a ruled place in it, and a heading over the place, for a clerk to fill. Nobody rules a place on a form for a thing that is always the same." She was speaking very fast now, and very small. "It is a class. It is the kind of thing that a certificate has, at its head, where it says what its bearer is. And I shall tell you one thing more, and then I shall stop, since I can see the edge of what I know. On the sheets of the fifth place, that square is not filled as ours are."
   178	
   179	"How is it filled?"
   180	
   181	"I shall draw it, exactly, and show it to nobody until I can say what I am showing. I have learned what comes of describing a mark before I can read it. I had two with and two without for the whole of a cold term, and I made nothing of them."
   182	
   183	She took the dead man's ledger, and opened it at its first page, and set it down above the four sheets, so that a small pressed stroke and bar in pencil lay over four in a clerk's ink.
   184	
   185	[[TIER-B: "Two copies of one ignorance." — the plan gives this line without its speaker; coordinator to supply the tag here]]
   186	
   187	Nobody at the slab asked who had filled that square, or by what rule, since there was not a person there who could have answered. Cael looked at five small marks. He had been stood on a plate once, and something had been written at the head of a sheet about him, in a place that a printer had ruled for it. It appeared that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He put that where he put the rest, and added no verb to it.
   188	
   189	Vastin had drawn breath, and Cael saw him shut his mouth on it. It was the first time that he had done so of himself.
   190	
   191	---
   192	
   193	It was a little after noon when she began to tie the tapes.
   194	
   195	"One thing more, before these are shut," said Karis. "I have a figure for every row, and I made each of them twice; but I made them both, and a count that one person has made is only a hope. I want a second hand on each. Take a row. Count its sheets, by the corners, and say nothing until you have the figure. Then I shall tell you mine."
   196	
   197	She gave the first row to Lira, who read everything with her lips and would count so. The third went to Brom, who dried his hands on his breeches a second time before he would touch it. Seln had gone to the basin, and stood there looking at the water with his back to the slab; she set the fourth by for him, and took the sixth herself, since it was the oldest and the softest.
   198	
   199	The second she slid across the rock to Cael.
   200	
   201	He looked at it. It lay in its loosened tape, a heap of printed sheets as thick as two fingers, and it was the first thing that anybody had put into his hands to be measured since a cord in a yard. He had been waiting all morning to be passed over. He saw now that she had not passed him over, and that she had done it on purpose: a small pale woman had thought about whether to give him a row to count, and had given it.
   202	
   203	"By the corners," she said, without looking up. "They are thin."
   204	
   205	He counted them. It was not hard. He lifted each sheet by its lower corner with one finger, and let it fall upon the last, and said the number in his head; the stock was soft, and it clung a little, and twice he went back three sheets to be sure that he had not lifted two together. He came to the row's foot, and to a figure.
   206	
   207	He did not trust it. He turned the whole heap over, face down, and counted it again from the back.
   208	
   209	"Lira," said Karis.
   210	
   211	Lira gave her figure.
   212	
   213	"That is mine. Brom."
   214	
   215	Brom gave his, and it was hers.
   216	
   217	"Cael."
   218	
   219	He told her what he had. He saw her pencil go to the place in the notebook, and stop there.
   220	
   221	"Again," she said, quite gently.
   222	
   223	"I've done it twice. From the front and from the back." His mouth had gone dry. "It's the same."
   224	
   225	"Then you are one short of me."
   226	
   227	The wind came round the end of the rock and lifted the corner of the uppermost sheet, and let it down. Nobody at the slab moved. Lira had turned her head; Brom had his thick fingers flat on the third row, as if it might be the next to go.
   228	
   229	Cael looked at his own two hands, which lay on either side of a heap of paper. It was a cord that he was thinking of, run out three times across an inn yard, with a slip of ash laid down at its end and a scratch on a stone trough under the middle of the slip. It was a hair, in a gap that had been measured for it. He had been one short then. He had held the thing exactly as four other people held it, and it had come out a piece under what it was.
   230	
   231	He had thought that paper would be different, since a sheet either lay in a heap or did not.
   232	
   233	"Count it for me," he said. "With your own hands. I'll not touch it."
   234	
   235	Karis had begun to rise from her stone. She had the look that he had seen on her in a store behind a chimney, with her cheek almost on the deal, and it was not unkind; it was a great deal worse than that. It was careful.
   236	
   237	A sleeve came past his shoulder.
   238	
   239	He had not heard anybody cross the turf. A small dry hand laid a single sheet down on the rock in front of him, between his two hands, and squared it with one finger, and went away again. It was thin stock, and newer than the rest, and it had been folded once. It had a printed head. At its foot a bar of black lay across the paper, as broad as a thumb, cracked like mud in a rut.
   240	
   241	It had not been laid at the foot of the second row, where it belonged, and it had not been given to Karis, who was the keeper of it. It had been set before him. And the man who had set it there did not say one word.

