/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 18 — A Year and a Day
     2	
     3	Seln had taken the sheet an hour before, from the foot of the second row, while a small pale woman was reading a dead man's four words aloud; and it had troubled him a little that it was still so easy.
     4	
     5	He had lifted papers out of heaps under their owners' eyes for the better part of his working life. One did not snatch. One was seen to be handling the heap for an honest reason, as he had been, reading six rows for what they left out. One's left hand squared the sheets while the right went on turning them. The sheet was in his sleeve before the pebble was back on its corner. Nobody at the slab had looked up. They were the only six people living from whom he had never before taken anything.
     6	
     7	He stood at the basin now, with his back to the rock, and held the thing low against his body, where the wind could not get at it.
     8	
     9	He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year and a day in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has copied the seat's paper for fifteen years does not see a station's date without turning it, any more than a carter sees a load without knowing what it weighs. The turning had been done before he knew that he was doing it. A day had come out.
    10	
    11	He had known that day. He had written it.
    12	
    13	It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
    14	
