     3	The fork-stone stood where the ruts parted, a slab of the dark rock as tall as Brom, set on end by people who had left no other mark on that moor.
     4	
     5	They came to it at dusk on the two hundred and eightieth day. One pair of ruts went on past it to the west, and began at once to climb a long bare rise that shut out the sky on that side. The other turned north, along the moor's edge, toward the blue hills. Somebody had cut a hand into each face of the stone, with the fingers pointing, and a hundred cold terms had worn both hands nearly smooth.
     6	
     7	The fold lay a little way down the northern branch, in a dip. It was a drovers' pen, round, of dry stone laid shoulder-high without a grain of mortar, and three parts of it were open to the sky. The fourth had been roofed with slabs. In that corner there was a hearth built into the wall, with a flue, and beside the hearth a stack of cut peat as high as a man's waist.
     8	
     9	"You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go, from the hag behind," said Oryn. "It's been the rule here since before the stone was stood up. A fire with a wall round it and a roof on it isn't on a road. I've slept here on thirty rounds."
    10	
    11	Brom built it, laying the peats on end as she showed him, and Karis set one point among them without being asked; and the thing caught slowly, with a sour brown smell that Cael had never met. It was the first flame that the seven of them had sat by since a hold on a shoulder, and only the second since the river.
    12	
    13	They did not all sit. Vastin stood in the gap of the fold, where the hurdle went, with his back against the stone and the bound leg out before him. He had come six miles from the spring on that stick, at the tail of the column, and had been offered a mule twice; and he had the grey look of a man who is counting what is left in him and finding it sufficient by a very little.
    14	
