   235	It was a long bare swell of the moor, a mile and more from its foot to its head. The ruts went up it aslant, as ruts do when they have been cut by loaded carts, and the wind came over the top of it into their faces. Nobody spoke, since it was a road, and the hold lay on all of them; and there was a box on three backs, and a man on a stick behind, who would not be waited for and could not be left.
   236	
