     1	# Chapter 4 — The Border Road
     2	
     3	The slip was on the plank when he came down on the morning of the two hundred and seventy-eighth day, and it had not been there the night before, and Pike, who had pinned it, did not look up when the six of them came in and read it, one after another, standing, as a crew reads a slip it already knows it is going to take.
     4	
     5	*Clearance. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road, two days west. Stillhound pack in the sink at the cut. Three carters lost since the thaw. Hold pays. Witness: the hold's factor.* And a price — twenty — which Brom read twice.
     6	
     7	"Border road," Lira said.
     8	
     9	"It's a road." Brom was still looking at the price. "It's the road."
    10	
    11	That was all the argument, and everyone at the plank heard it for what it was. The border road ran west from the ford three days to the Line and the city on it, and it was the only road that did, and a slip on Pike's board that paid a crew to walk two days of it was a slip that paid a crew to walk two days toward Treswick, and nobody at the plank had said *Treswick* aloud since a page had been set on a table's bare end, and nobody was going to say it now, because saying it was a decision and taking a slip was a job.
    12	
    13	"Ours," Karis said, before Brom could.
    14	
    15	Brom looked at her. Then he looked at Pike, and said, "Ours," because a price was a thing Brom said, and Pike wrote HOLD and the five names under it in the order he always wrote them, and then, because the board wrote what it saw and Pike had seen a healer's roll come through his door on the two hundred and sixty-eighth day and had not seen it go, he wrote a sixth line under the five, shorter, and did not write a seventh.
    16	
    17	Cael read the sixth line the wrong way round. It was a healer's mark and a route's, which was how Pike wrote a woman who was a stop on her own circuit, and it was ink, and a thing Pike wrote was a thing.
    18	
    19	At the door end, the man in the brown coat had watched the six of them read a slip and take it in two words, both of them *Ours*, and had not moved, and was not on the sheet, and picked up his stick when they went out, and came.
    20	
    21	---
    22	
    23	He catalogued the border road because it was the first road he had walked in nine months that somebody kept.
    24	
    25	It ran west from the ford along the river's north bank for a mile and then left the river and went up through the scrub to the first shoulder, and it was a road as the void roads were not: a surface, laid and mended, with the winter's ruts filled in with broken stone by somebody paid to fill them; a waypost every mile, a squared timber with a mark cut in it, the marks counting down toward the Line; a ditch on the uphill side that somebody had cleared of last year's leaves. A kept road. He had walked kept roads for four years inside the Line and had stopped seeing them, and nine months of ruts had taught him to see one again, and he did not like what he saw, which was a country that measured its roads.
    26	
    27	Seven of them on it. Six, and the mule, and a man with a stick who walked at the back of the column where a man walks who has not been told where to walk and has decided that the back is where he can do the least harm. Oryn walked beside the mule with her hand on its rope and her roll on her back and the road west under her feet, which was not her road — her road went north from a fork on the far side of the shoulder hold, seven holds on it, and she had said nothing about the fork and nobody had asked her, because that was the condition. Lira walked ahead, on her own foot, with two arms swinging, thirteen days released and still, Cael saw, putting the left hand into her coat and taking it out again to make sure it went. Brom with the shoulder carried and the kit on the other one. Karis with the records flat in their oilcloth under her arm and the map in her coat and the two brass edges in her pocket, walking as she had walked the void roads with a straightedge open in her hand, looking at the ground ahead as if a line were drawn on it, which, on this road, there was: somebody had drawn it a long time ago, with instruments, and somebody else had been mending it since.
    28	
    29	Seln ahead of Lira, and then not.
    30	
    31	The first day was road and nothing else, and he wrote it as that at the halt, and slept, and the second day was the cut.
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	The shoulder hold was a hold on a shoulder above the road, walled, with a gate that shut, and a factor at the gate who had lost three carters since the thaw and said so in the voice a man uses for a thing that has cost him money and will cost him more.
    36	
    37	"The cut," he said. He pointed down the road, west, where it left the shoulder and went into a fold of the hills. "Half a mile. The road goes through a cut in the rock — it's the only way down off this shoulder that a cart can take — and there's a sink on the north side, below the road, where the runoff pools, and it's frozen still, in there, this late, because the sun doesn't get in. They've denned in the sink. They come up onto the road when a cart goes through." He looked at the seven of them and the mule. "Carters declare, on the ice. You know that? And a den beside a road learns the road — lately they come up at whatever crosses, and most of what crosses declares. A man's cart slides on the ice in the cut and he throws something at the wheel to hold it — a Path, whatever he's got — and that's what they come for. Three carts. Three men. We found the carts."
    38	
    39	"How many," Seln said.
    40	
    41	"Seven. Eight. Nobody's stood still long enough to count."
    42	
    43	Seln nodded, once, as if seven or eight were a number he could work with, which it was.
    44	
    45	They went down to look at it from above before they went into it, because Cael had learned at fourteen that a thing you walked into without looking at first was a thing that looked at you, and he catalogued it from the lip of the shoulder with the six of them beside him and the man with the stick a pace back.
    46	
    47	A cut. The road went into the rock where the shoulder broke, straight, between two walls of grey stone that somebody had cut back long ago to take a cart's width and a hand on either side, a hundred strides of it, falling toward the west. Down the middle of the road, where the road's crown should have been, a runnel — the winter's runoff, frozen, a ribbon of grey ice a stride wide that had not thawed because the walls kept the sun off it. The walls themselves not sheer: scree, on both sides, steep, loose, the kind of slope a body could go up on hands and feet and could not go up fast. And on the north side, halfway along, below the road's level where the wall broke and fell away, the sink — a hollow in the rock the size of a hold's yard, floored with ice, in shadow, with the dark mouths in its far side where the runoff had cut under the stone and made a place a grey thing could lie down in.
    48	
    49	He drew it in his head as Karis would have drawn it: a line, the cut; a ribbon down the line, the ice; the sink a bowl off the line's side, fifty meters below and north; the scree the line's two banks. The pack would come up out of the bowl onto the line. The line was a stride and a hand wide with ice down its middle. Anything on the line was on the ice or beside it.
    50	
    51	"They come up onto the road," Lira said. "From the sink. Below us, north side, halfway."
    52	
    53	"Onto the ice," Brom said.
    54	
    55	"Onto the ice." Lira looked at the runnel. "They can run on it. We can't."
    56	
    57	"We don't run on it," Cael said. "Nobody's on the ice. Brom's on the road above the sink, on the stone, south side, with a rock. Lira's on the scree, south wall, above him — the slope's slow for them too. Seln's where he'll be. Karis and Oryn at the top of the cut, east end, behind us, and the mule. I'm on the road, west of the sink, past it, so that when they come up they've a choice between the thing on the road that's moving and the thing on the road that isn't." He looked at the man with the stick. "Vastin."
    58	
    59	"I'm listening."
    60	
    61	"They hunt discharge. A Path is a shout. A still body is nearly nothing to them and a declaration is everything, and they turn to the loudest one, all of them, at once, and they follow whoever moves first." He said it as Karis had said it at a ford in the autumn, as a finding, because it was one. "We fight them silent. Nobody declares unless it's on purpose and unless they're the one who's meant to be loud. Do you understand what I'm telling you?"
    62	
    63	"You're telling me not to fight."
    64	
    65	"I'm telling you how this crew fights. I don't know how you fight. I've only ever seen you stand."
    66	
    67	Vastin looked at the cut for a while. "Where do you want me."
    68	
    69	"Behind Brom. South side. On the stone." He paused. "And still."
    70	
    71	"I can be still," Vastin said. "It's the one thing I've been trained to."
    72	
    73	Oryn had said nothing. She had looked at the cut, and at the sink, and at the scree, and Cael saw her arrive at the place where the casualties would be, which was the road's south edge above the sink, where Brom would stand, and file it; and then she took the mule's rope, and went with Karis to the cut's east end, and stood where a healer stands, which is not in it.
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	They walked the cut silent.
    78	
    79	The silence was a thing he had learned on the void roads and had thought he would not need on a kept one, and he counted it now as he counted anything he needed. The seven of them in the order he had set, with the ice down the road's middle and the walls close, and nobody's boot on the ice, and nobody's breath loud, and the scree ticking once under Lira as she went up it and then not ticking, and the sink below the road's north edge, halfway, in shadow, with the dark mouths in its far side, and nothing coming out of them.
    80	
    81	Forty strides. Fifty. Brom at his place above the sink, on the stone, south side, with a rock the size of a loaf that he had found at the cut's mouth and carried in, and his shoulder still and forward and his other arm loose. Vastin behind him, a stride back, on the stone, with the stick, still. Cael past the sink now, west of it, twenty strides down the road, on the south edge with the ice at his left boot, turned to face back up the cut, alone, which was the point.
    82	
    83	Nothing.
    84	
    85	Then the pack came up out of the sink the way water comes up out of a hole, all at once and without a sound, grey and low, over the road's north edge and onto the ice, eight of them, and turned — every head at once, the way the ford had taught him and the Ring had taught him twice — toward the loudest thing in the cut.
    86	
    87	The loudest thing in the cut was a wall.
    88	
    89	He had not seen an Iron Wall go up. He had read the Path's name on a page at fifteen and had stood in front of the man who carried it for four years and had never once seen it used, because the Compact's senior enforcement officer did not need to use anything in a hall. It went up now, two feet from Brom's back, across the road, wall to wall, the width of the cut — the road's width; the scree above it was not road — not a thing he could see, exactly; a *plane*, a place where the air had been told it was stone, and had believed it — and all the pack, on the ice, all eight, turned from the road and the moving boy and the man with the rock, and went for the wall.
    90	
    91	It held. He catalogued that first, because it was the first fact: eight stillhounds hit a plane of nothing at a run, on ice, and stopped as if they had hit the cut's own rock, and the two in front went down and the six behind went over them, and the plane did not give a hair. A wall. Ground that did not give. He had watched Brom go hard a hundred times and had never seen a thing like it: Brom was a man made harder; this was a *line*, drawn across a road, and nothing on the road was going past it.
    92	
