editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:161:He had gone out at noon on the first full day of their stay, with the witnessed slip in his hat and nothing in the cart, sitting on the tailboard as he had not once sat while the grain was on it. An empty cart behind a rested mule, it seemed, could do in one day what a loaded one did in two. He had brought salt up with him, and a keg of nails, and a bolt of blue cloth that three women came out of the house to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:267:There was an iron box bolted to the nearer post, under the plank, with a lock on it the size of a fist. Pike opened it with a key from round his neck and took out two coins, and laid them on the oak in front of Brom, who happened to be standing nearest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael had not known that he ever did. He walked across the yard in his slow dry way with a length of ash in his hand, peeled, as tall as his shoulder, with a fork left at the top of it for a palm to sit in, and he stood it against the trough where Lira's hand would find it. He kept his eyes off her foot; he lifted two fingers from his side, an inch, and let them fall, and went back up his ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:249:He had walked the east road on the ninth evening, within an hour of stepping off a ferry, as far as a saddle and a little beyond it, and the north before light on the tenth. The south road had cost him two mornings, since it forked. The fourth was the road they had all come in by, and he had gone back along that one too, three miles, to see it as a person sees it who is arriving. This was the fifth. It left the north road at a dead tree and climbed, and the meadow called it cold; he learned why at the first turn, where a wind came down off bare ground that had no business in such mild weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:"That's been on my oak since the thaw," he said. "It's the carters' well, at the fork, where the south road splits. It went sour. A lad was sent down after a bucket, and they wound him up asleep; he woke, and he's not been right about a ladder since." He drew five strokes on the bark. "Nobody's been down it; they water at a stream a mile on, and curse me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:167:They went out on the sixty-second, slowly. The south road ran level through river meadows, and the leg that had given eleven strides at Thornwater gave twenty now and wanted a shorter rest. Lira would not ride anything; she went the whole way on the ash stick with her mouth shut, and at noon they came to the fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:331:A carter came up the south fork with two mules while the last bucket stood on the curb. He stopped them at the edge of the halting-ground and stood watching Brom, plainly waiting to learn whether a large young man would drop where he stood. When a full minute had gone by he came and dipped his own cup, and then he scratched a wheel on the slip with the point of his knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:339:"*The well at the south fork. Air cleared, shaft drawn, six stones set. Four marks.*" He said it for the tents, and wrote it, and said it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:345:It was the bowman's ash, and the fork at its top had gone pale and smooth where her palm sat. She turned it over once when Pike had done, and looked at it, and then she walked to the inn wall, beside the left-hand post, and stood the stick against the plaster; she set it upright, with some care, as one leaves a borrowed tool where the next person will want it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:Oryn had loaded the mule in the hollow. Two of the boxes hung from the pack-saddle, one on either flank, and her own panniers were lashed on top with the crossbow in its cover. She took the animal by the cheek-strap and walked it to the edge of the break, and there she changed her hand and put herself on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"If she slips with me on the inside, she goes that way with nothing to stop her. This way she's got something to lean on." Oryn clicked her tongue. "She's a mule, and she'd sooner lean than fall. Most things would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:233:So the five of them stood under the long eave, and waited to be paid. Nobody in the meadow came near. But a woman frying dough had stopped with her fork in the air when they came by, and she had not yet gone back to her pan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:It took Brom three strides' worth of standing still. He set his boot in the stirrup and bent, with the box riding up his shoulders, and drew. When the string dropped home he made a small sound through his nose. It had opened his fingers. Cael could not see how deeply, in that light; he saw only that Brom held one hand away from himself for a breath, as a man holds it after picking up something hot. Then he laid a bolt in the groove out of the sleeve on the stock. That left four in the sleeve, and one in a pale thing on the floor behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:313:Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. Four of the six were hurt, and he would have the count of that by lamplight. He knew already that every hurt but his own had been taken by somebody who had no means of answering. Nine things lay under his hand again, and they had not yet stopped shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Oryn was waiting at the east gate with the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:37:They came to the fork an hour out. The valley divided there round a spur of hill, and one road went away up the left-hand water toward a smoke that he could just see. The right-hand branch climbed to a low place in the skyline and went over. It was a road like any other, two pale ruts with a ridge of grass between. There was nothing at its mouth to say what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:"That's the Fallow," said Oryn, and clicked her tongue to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:169:"That's it," said Oryn. She had halted the mule. "I've never been nearer than these ruts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:249:The mule had settled the matter of the line for herself an hour before. She had been left with her rein under a rock, and she had drawn it out and grazed her way down the old road; and when Oryn went to fetch her she was standing with her forefeet on one side of the grey stone and her hind feet on the other, pulling at the grass, as easy as a beast in its own paddock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:79:"I've been at that since the fork in the road," said Cael. "I'll give you what I've got, and there are three reasons."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:109:It was the question that she had put by on a shelf at a stone; he saw that this was the same question, cut to a smaller size, and that she had every right to this one tonight. He had known the answer since the fork, and had gone round it for the rest of that day as a carter goes round a ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with four fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:33:"Ninety-sixth day. Dark. The west side of the stone. There were nine. Two are dead, one on the floor with a bolt in it that we'll not see again, and one there." He did not point. "Seven went out when the wall lit, north and south, along the outside of the line. I didn't see where they went after. Did anyone?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:37:"Four of us are hurt, and she'll have each of those from the one that it belongs to. Six bolts, at noon: three in the sleeve, one on the string, one a yard from that stone, and one gone. The box is whole, and there are eleven sheets in it and on her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:163:Brom had come in. He did not wait to be asked. He knelt down heavily beside the sleeping healer, box and all, and laid the bow in the grass with the bolt still on it, and held out one hand into the light, palm up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:201:"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:107:Oryn stood looking at the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:145:He was the hold's boy, twelve or so and all elbows. He put his boot in the stirrup, and a hook on his belt over the string, and straightened his back with a grunt; and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch. Then he laid a bolt in the groove. It was a stubby thing with a blunt wooden head and three goose feathers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:147:So that was how it would be. She would never loose a bolt that somebody else had not bent his back for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:163:She loosed. The stock kicked against her collarbone, and the bolt went over the sack by the height of a man and stuck in the frozen grass a long way beyond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:167:She shot eight. The boy bent his back eight times. Three of them hit the sack somewhere, and not one was in the ring; and at the end of it the boy went out through the wicket in the gate, in the one hour of the day when nothing had ever been seen on that valley, and gathered eight bolts out of the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:171:She knew what to do with eight bolts. She counted them back into the boy's hand, one at a time, and said that she would be up at noon tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:15:The sun stood as high as it would go at that time of year, pale and small, and their shadows lay short on the frosted grass. They had gathered on the western side of the grey stone, with the turn of the ruts at their backs and the ring two hundred yards and more in front. Oryn had the mule by the cheek-strap a few yards behind the rest, and her free hand tucked into her armpit for warmth; she had watched the whole of the morning from that stone without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Cael watched it happen. Five people had each lost a thing, a stride from where they stood, and had it handed back, and every one of them had turned inward to look at it. Oryn was hobbling the mule, with her lips moving in a count that she did not seem to know she was keeping. Brom knelt by the middle box. Karis sat with her eyes shut, and Lira walked up and down on the great boot, a little apart, and Seln stood looking east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:45:"There's a bow on that mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:"I looked at it at noon and left it lying. I'd come to find out about my hands, and I didn't want anything in them." Her mouth went hard. "That was a healer's reason. Six bolts, in a sleeve on the stock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:257:Seln drew a bolt from the sleeve on the stock, and laid it in the groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:15:He stood there. Seln cut the bolt out first, since there would be no getting at it afterward. He did it with the narrow knife and his back turned to the rest of them, and it took him very little time; when he straightened he had a short thick shaft in his hand with the iron unbent, and he wiped it on the grass and held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:17:Karis had the sleeve. She had taken charge of it at noon, when Brom was told what he might not do, and she slid the bolt in beside the rest and showed them the mouth of the leather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:*Ninety-seventh day. The stone. Two mended at noon, and nothing came. My calf read a fourth time, and left in its cloth. Five bolts. Bread in sixths, for four meals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:It was a mile to the fork. He walked it at a mule's shoulder with a whole leg, and for the first time in fifteen days he let himself look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:They came down to the fork an hour after noon, and up the valley with the smoke of a hold standing over the fence in front of them. The east gate was open. He had gone out by it five days before with five other people and a calfskin, to pick up something that somebody had left lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:371:It was the same yard. He had not looked at it when he came in. There was the sweep of the well, and the cart-yard, and a boy forking straw into the barn under the loft where two boxes waited; the geese went by in a line, and somebody in the long house was scouring a pot. He had gone out of this place five days ago and stood on a floor that matched no stone in the world, and held a hound's head, and carried a light down a hill. Nothing here had altered by so much as a goose, and he found that he had been counting on that without knowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:285:They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:41:"A bolt, six days gone. I drew it and shut it on a road. Then he came off a wall yesterday and hung by it, and I shut it again, in that ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:99:"There's a man with them who had a bolt through his shoulder," Teague told her. "Their healer shut it that night, with her hands, on the grass. She's a good one; I've seen her work. He walked off next day with a case on his other side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:"South. Past the well you cleared, and up onto the ridge. It's a carter's day to Millrace by it, and the road forks above the mill." Pike rubbed the stump of his arm through the pinned sleeve. "One branch drops to the ford. The other keeps the ridge half a day more, and comes off the end of it, and the carts go round what's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:261:The bolt went into the pale to its feathers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:On the kerb, which was the hall's outer coping, Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:325:Nobody proposed going back for anything. They left it in its water, with four of a healer's bolts in it, and went out along the grey path in file, at one pace, as they had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:19:He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:31:*One.* Everything that he owned went dumb. For the length of that count there was a boy standing on a hillside who could not have shifted a foot to save his life; and he spent it looking up at the brow, since his eyes were the only part of him that would answer. The man with the bow had not stood to watch his bolt. He had the nose of the thing on the turf already, and his boot going into the stirrup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:When he came down he had a crossbow in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:181:"There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:185:Cael had known him for two years. He had seen him carve a goat, and wax a seal, and stand in a doorway with his back to a room, and he had never seen him do a thing in anger. Seln lifted the crossbow over his head and brought it down across the grey stone with the whole of his narrow strength, and the stock broke in two at the lock. He stood over it, breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:193:*Hundred and fourteenth day, night. Outside the stone. All six out. Brom: a bolt through the right shoulder, drawn and mended, an hour. Lira: the arm opened on the stair wall and is shut again, a quarter. Thirteen bolts loosed at us in the day, and one hit. Ours: five, and none shot.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:197:*Anchor: looking, at the two bars, first light. I saw the fence go up. I stood two counts to see it, and Brom had those two counts with a bolt in him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:233:He was of the middle height, and heavy in the forearm, and there was a band of black leather laced on his right wrist. He stopped at the edge of their lamplight, and Cael saw his eyes go round it once, slowly. They went to a big man lying on his left side under a healer's coat, and to a girl whose sling was tied a hand higher than it had been at a certain door. They went to two pieces of a crossbow lying side by side by a grey stone. They came to Cael, and to his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:283:They walked at noon, and slept at the fork above the mill, without a fire, a crew's length apart; and before he lay down he counted his three by their breathing, as he did every night of his life. The bow's was the short one, since that hip had not liked the ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:285:It was the boy who asked, at that fork, before the blankets were down. He had a heel of bread half way to his mouth, and he stopped it there, as he stopped eating whenever his captain was about to speak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:351:That was a crossbow. He had expected a bow of some kind. He had not expected that every sign of its owner would stop at the height of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:He sent the spear south-about by the ruts with the lantern, and the boy with him. They were gone the better part of two hours. They came back by the north, having walked the whole of the ruts, with a crossbow each and a third across the boy's shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:27:"It rode on my mule for thirty rounds in a cover, and I looked at it at noon one day, at a stone, and left it where it was. It's done more across your knee than it ever did for me." Oryn did not put out a hand. "There's one in it. See that it stays in till it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:63:*Hundred and twenty-second day, the fork. Oryn north. On her terms. I didn't ask. Five on the bearing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:49:"He has the hole. He has not the stair. That bolt came in at the top and spent itself on thirty steps of stone; it would not have gone through a coat when it reached her." Seln had not raised his voice. "He knows that as well as I do. He wished us to know that he can reach the door. It is a kind of knock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:107:"I have had since this morning to read it, and you should have my reading. The bolt she read for herself, on the road, and she read it rightly. A crew with a hurt man and a healer at the stone might have fought its way back to her. A crew with a hurt man and a healer who has no hands will sit still." His mild voice did not alter. "There are six of them, for a rim of sixteen hundred paces. That is a quarter of a mile to each station, and nobody to spare, which is why they have not come down; if one leaves his place, there is a gap in the dish that five people might run for. They will not come in, and they do not need to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:227:He had made up his mind about the next thing in the dark. There were ten bolts lying on that grey somewhere, where they had skated and stopped, and one kick would send any of them ringing; so he gave the read the ground, which was the oldest and quietest thing that he owned, and it went out under his soles for its pace and a half, as it always did. It told him what was there, and did not tell him what anything meant. But a spent bolt has a weight, and he felt the first of them lying a yard ahead and a little to the left, like a pencil on a table, before his boot could find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:231:It took the whole of his attention. He was a boy walking across thirty yards of bare stone in the last of the light, with six people looking at that stone; and he could not raise his eyes to them. He had them in his head only: two on the brow in front of him, two away on his left, one on his right, and one behind, where the west was still green. He walked with his eyes in the ground, and stepped over four bolts, and went round a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:It was Seln's hour, and Cael had given it to him without argument. A loaded cart took the whole of a short day to reach the mill; a crew that walked as they walked could be on the ridge by sunrise and at the fork before noon, and Seln wished to have the afternoon in hand. So Brom loaded the mule by feel. Lira ate standing. They went past Pike's bare plank and the smith's cold chimney, and took the south road in file, with the frost ringing under five pairs of boots and four small hooves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:They passed the well at the fork in the dark. It had a new rope on its windlass, and somebody had mended the curb where six stones had been set; and Cael, who had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, put his hand on the stonework as he went by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:19:"The fork," said Seln. He had walked the first hours of that road by himself, in their second week at Lowmarch, and had never said what he found. "The left hand is ours, later. The right is the mill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:He gave it in order and left nothing out: a line that was said twice at a hearth, and a nail, and a sheet of cream paper that had lain sealed in a broker's box beside the first. He gave her the sum on it. He told her what Seln had found in its margin, and what the five of them had made of it at a fire, and that one of the five had said walk away and been entered. He said that the place was half a day up the ridge from the fork over her head, and that he meant to be at its edge by dusk and across it in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:239:"She's a mule. She knows where the hay used to come from." Oryn took up the rope. "You'll keep on the low side of her, as I told you. I'll go behind with this one, where she can't see the load and want it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:At the fork there was a flat rock, and Cael sat down on it with Hesk's book while the two mules were let to blow. The grey had come up the notch backward for a good part of the way. The mill's wheel was still to be heard below them, very small. He wrote under what he had written on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:149:There were seven of them under the back room's window in the morning, in the frost, and the first was sitting bolt upright on the bench's end with a stick across her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:203:And there was what a round of three weeks had cost, which was the sum that all five of them would have been doing since a fork. It was not a kindness to tell them. It was a finding, and she gave people their findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:There was the other thing, for which he had no good word. He had held a point against a pair of jaws while four bolts went in, and it had taken everything else that he owned to do it; not one of the ten fragments had yet come back to where it lived, and he felt like a house from which the furniture has been carried out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:175:"You'll have the mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:223:The bolt stood in the sack, a hand outside the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:229:She put four of the eight into the ring. He counted them, and so did a good many other people, though nobody on that ground said a figure aloud; Karis, behind him, was making strokes in her notebook with the pencil held very small. The boy went out between each pair and came back at a run, with his chin up, as if the bolts had been his own. Not one went over the sack, and not one fell short of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:237:It was the only one that he gave her. Cael understood it: there was to be a single bolt from the knee, at sixty paces, with a hold looking on, and no second if it went wide. Wind had come up in his feet to go with her, as it had on a rim; and he heard it come, and let it lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:245:The bolt was in the ring, low, and a little left of the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:25:The foot was taped, from a roll that had been put into her hand at a fork, without a word, by a person who had then walked north; and she wound it herself each morning, sitting on a box, with her one hand and her teeth. Then she set the great boot down whole at every stride of every mile, on ruts that were iron and on turf that was glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:17:Brom came last. Cael heard him grunt at the stride, as he had grunted on the Fallow, like a man who has felt a door shut behind him. The healer's bow was on his left shoulder, spanned, with a bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:45:He was twelve feet from the stone when he made up his mind. He had refused Anchor, the thinnest of his ten, at these two irons the evening before, and again in the frost an hour ago. He let it look now, on purpose, with his eyes open; and since he had stopped dead to do it, he stood on a frozen road for the length of two counts while a friend with a bolt in him was being turned about by the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:77:She understood him. He saw her understand all of it at once, as she understood a break under her hands: the bolt, and where it had been put, and for whom. Her face did not change. She shifted Brom's arm higher on her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:"That was for me, then," said Oryn. "That bolt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:There were two on the brow to the east, where the old road came over. One had a crossbow, with its nose on the ground and his boot in the stirrup; he was drawing the string with a hook, steadily, like a man doing a thing that he had done ten thousand times. The other stood two yards south of him and had nothing in his hands at all, and his arms were coming down to his sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:He held them. He stood on a frozen road, with a bolt in Brom's shoulder and a healer staring at her own hand, and refused nine things one after another in the only country on the continent where every one of them would have answered. He had done as much once before, on a white floor, with a girl's arm in a hound's mouth. It was no lighter. Seln had told him that it would not be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:She had seen it on the Fallow from a foot away, and she saw it now from three yards. He watched her know what he was doing, and why, and what it was going to cost somebody before the day was out. Then her eyes left his face, and went up to the brow, where a man had got his string behind its catch and was reaching to his belt for a bolt. They went south, and west, and north, right round, in the time of one breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:She did not wait to be answered. She stepped in close under Brom's good arm, and with her right hand she lifted the healer's bow off his left shoulder by its stock, spanned as it was, with the bolt held in the groove under her thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:Karis was away first, with her coat held up in both fists. Brom ran. A bolt in the shoulder is not a bolt in the leg, and he went down that road like a bullock, with the feathers of the bolt nodding at his shoulder and the healer at his left hand, matching him stride for stride with her fingers hooked in his belt. Seln ran a yard to his right. Cael came last, since somebody must, and since if any of them fell he wished to be the one who saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:At sixty the bolt came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:223:"They are two hundred and sixty yards from that hole, on every side, and very little above it. A bolt that comes in at the top must come in nearly level. It strikes the wall or the upper steps." Seln turned round. "From the rim, nobody can see into this. Nobody can see so much as the fourth step. And anybody who comes to the edge of the hole to look down is a black shape on a bright square, to a person sitting where you are sitting, with that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:239:She had cut his coat away from the shoulder with Seln's knife. Karis held the lamp where she was told. In its light the thing was plain enough, and Cael made himself look at it, since it was his crew's: the bolt had gone in behind, a hand below the top of the shoulder, and its iron head stood out in front, under the collar-bone, by the length of a thumb. There was not much blood. What there was came slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:263:"I can see what's wrong. I can't feel it and I can't touch it. Tape it. Leave the bolt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:267:"It's high. It's missed the lung and it's missed the big vessel, or he'd not be talking. Leave it in, because the bolt's the plug. When my hands work again I'll take it out and close what it opened. Not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:61:The bolt lay where it had stopped, on the thirty-first step, with its three grey feathers turned to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:She had said at the stone that she would not sit outside a second day and listen. Four people who knew what a crossbow was were holding the rim, and she meant to be where a certain shoulder was; if anything opened, it could be carried up forty steps and two hundred yards with her walking beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:None of them had been. They had seen that room once, for as long as a healer needed a lamp, with a bolt in one of them; and Cael went round it now as he would have liked to then. The roof was the underside of the floor that they had crossed, without a beam or a crack, and when he held the lantern up to it he found his own light looking back at him dully, as from still water. The floor was the same grey. There was a dust on it as thin as the bloom on a plum, which had taken their boots of yesterday along the northern wall and beside the door, and nobody's anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:331:"It was forty steps yesterday, with a bolt in, and I counted them." He looked at the three of them, and then at the healer, who had opened her mouth and shut it again. "It's the only thing that we ever fetched for a paper and found, and I'd like to be the one that brings it up. I didn't do a great deal else down here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:He had been over the floor, and through the grass below the stone, and he had an armful of ash shafts, which he laid on the turf in a row beside four crossbows, one of which was in two pieces. Lira drew the one from her belt and laid it at the end of the row. There was an iron head lying by the stone that had no shaft to it; the boy looked at that, and at Brom, and went rather red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:316:"It comes up when you talk, at the end of a sentence, and you've always done it." Cael sat down on the wall. "The declaration says *either foot lifts.* A heel is part of a foot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:380:Joren slid off the wall, set his feet on the paving, and stood there, flat-footed and stubborn, in front of a wall that did not hold anything up. Cael could see in his shoulders that he meant to stand there for the whole hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-11.md:149:The Stone woman, for her part, had begun to set her feet a fraction later each time, so that there was a moment, at the start of every stance, when she was not quite rooted yet. He saw that too. Cael watched them both see it, one after the other, and he watched them both try to use what they had seen, and it was like watching two people play a board game who had each just noticed the other's mistake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:He was glad of it, which surprised him. The first hours out of Denvash had been farmland: stubble and plough on both hands, low stone walls dividing it, a farmhouse every mile or so with its morning smoke going straight up. Carts had passed him going the other way into the city with cabbages and split wood. A family had gone by on foot, pulling a handcart with a baby asleep among the sacks, and two merchants on horseback had overtaken him at a trot without once looking down. None of it had felt like company. Every walk he had ever taken had been inside a city that answered him. Each street had a name, each distance stopped somewhere, and round every corner was a thing he already knew. Out here the road simply kept going and had no opinion about him. The only regular thing in the whole landscape was the sound of his own boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:11:From the brick lip on the southern edge, if you leaned out a little, you could see the corner where Fen Street met the canal path, and the low wall. Joren was standing in front of it with his feet planted and his arms folded. Even from here Cael could see how still he was holding himself, flat-footed and stubborn in front of a wall that did not hold anything up. Every few minutes he unfolded his arms and looked up Fen Street, toward Cael's house, and then folded them again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-21.md:113:The cut was very quiet. He could hear the river below, and the cooper's mallet beginning on the other side of the wall, slow, like somebody knocking at a door who did not expect an answer. He could hear his own breath. At forty a cat came down the steps from above, saw him in the doorway, stopped with one foot lifted, and went back up the way it had come with great dignity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-31.md:113:He had gone back to Part Four, which said what a compliance evaluation was, because everything else had come to walls. He had read it once already, in the first hour, and found nothing in it but a definition. He read it again now, slowly, every word, and when he came to the end of the section he did not turn the page. He looked at the foot of it instead, at the band of very small print below the last line, where the book kept its notes. He had stopped reading the notes some time in the second hour because there were so many of them and they were so small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:It was not a long stop, only the length of a swallow: his chest went still, and his mouth shut, and a half-beat later the heat came. Cael felt it from the rope, on his face, like an oven door opened across a kitchen, and the Force youth flinched sideways out of it and lost his footing on the groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-38.md:121:The gate in the wall was open and the frost was white along the foot of the east wall, and in the grey half, at the post, somebody was striking the canvas, low, twice, and stepping round it, and striking again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-46.md:149:"Not here," said Feryn. "You stand like a city. Somewhere with walls." He shifted his weight a hair from one foot to the other. "I've fought in a city with walls. Hated it. Everybody watches you from the top."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:323:It did not work well. It worked so badly that anybody watching would have thought he was trying to step over something on the floor and had misjudged it. It was clumsy and slow, and nothing like the drop under her backhand, or the going across against Dessa, which had come out of him whole and perfect and not his. But for a moment, for one step, the boards between the cot and the wall were very slightly less firm about how far apart they were. It was the width of a hand, perhaps less: the space between where his foot had been and where it landed was a little shorter than it should have been, and he felt it go short under him, like a stair that is not quite the height you expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:He sat on the wall for an hour, and it did not finish. At the end of the hour he made the Wind step once, on the frozen flags, to be sure he still could, and it came, clumsily, the stones going a hand's width short under his foot. So it was not the asking. He had asked twice tonight, and one thing had answered and the other had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-57.md:151:It was what Feryn had shown him on the wall. The right foot came forward, light, and rested. The left went back and down, and set, and then set again, a small second tap of the heel as though to be sure of it. The hands hung loose. And the weight went down, all of it, lower and lower, until the man in front of him looked less like somebody who had walked into the yard than like something the yard had been built round, the way the old man's grey post stood in the middle of his square of dirt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-56.md:123:Cael had a bill of the mender's in his pocket, for washers, and a stub of pencil. He turned the bill over on the wall and drew a man on the back of it as Corvane had taught him to draw a man, a stick figure, with the right foot forward and the left foot back and planted. Then he drew a line from the right shoulder, where a strike might land, across the chest and down. He drew another from the right ribs, across. Another from the jaw, down the neck and across. Another from the left shoulder, straight down. And all of them, if they were going back and down toward a left foot nailed to the floor, had to go through the same narrow place to get there: the low left side, where the ribs stopped and the hip began. Below that there was only one leg for any of it to go down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-51.md:277:"Saturday." The old man shifted his weight, very slightly, from one foot to the other, as he did. "When you can come up this lane without holding the wall. Bring a coin for yard time." He looked at Lira, briefly, and back at Cael. "Not her. You."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:11:He went up to the old man's yard on both Saturdays with a coin. The coin went on the post. On the first Saturday the old man had him stand for half an hour with a finger on his breastbone and say, every time he felt himself holding with more than he needed, *there*. On the second he had him do nothing at all for an hour but walk round the inside of the yard wall, slowly. The old man stood in his corner and said, every so often, without explanation, *shoulders*, or *jaw*, or *you're holding your breath again, what for?* Cael walked home both times more tired than after a bout, and he could not have said why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-52.md:127:On Saturday he went up the lane of yards without holding the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:125:That would do. It went faster when he was frightened, but he was not frightened of Brom. It went faster when he ran, but he would be sitting still. He counted it against the shadow of the lamp-flame moving on the wall, for long enough to trust it. Then he wrote *pulse* at the foot of the middle column, and went to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:93:It was the last of the ideas he had brought with him. The lock held him rooted to both feet. But if, in the air, he set his weight a little more onto his right foot than his left, perhaps when the lock let go he would be already leaning, already half moved, and could push off it the instant it opened instead of a beat after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:119:He went at her. The floor went short, and the half body's width that usually took him round her took him straight into her instead, and he landed so close that his chest bumped her staff where she held it across herself, and locked there. She had been ready for him to land somewhere. She had not been ready for him to land on her. She tried to step back to get room to swing and found his foot on hers, and pushed at him with the staff, and he could not move to be pushed, and the two of them leaned on each other like two drunk men holding up a wall. On the second *and* the lock let go and his weight came off her foot all at once, and she went backward and he went forward, and they both sat down hard on the stone in a tangle of staff and knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:She stood behind the bench with her arms folded on the top of the alcove wall and her chin on her arms, and said nothing for most of the hour. Brom had the newcomers again, the boy with the tooth and the girl and the tall lad, and two more from Keth's ring whom Cael had not seen before. He gave each of them a copper and let them hit him, and sent them sideways, and paid the next. Cael counted on his wrist and wrote in the middle column. Behind him Lira breathed slowly through her nose, as she did when she was reading somebody, and once, when the tall lad's kick came back past him off the edge of Brom's foot, she made a small sound in her throat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:139:He lost them all the same way, and it took him a while to understand it, because nobody at home had ever let him lose often enough to see the shape. The wall held. It held every time. But it cost. Holding it all over at once was like paying rent on every room of a house when you only ever sat in one of them. It was a slow, even draw from somewhere under his ribs that did not hurt at all until, quite suddenly, there was nothing left to draw. The men in the boat shed did not need to break the wall; they only needed to keep him holding it. They went round him, tapping, testing, never committing to anything he could catch, until somewhere about the fifth or sixth exchange the wall came down by itself because he had nothing left to hold it with. Then they hit him, and they meant it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Cael was alone in Lira's alcove with his back against the wall, working the short right-hand steps he had been working since the kitchen table, a dozen and then a dozen more, slow, with his eyes shut so that he could feel how late the right foot was against the left. The lamp in the north window was out, because Lira had come up the hill a little after two and he had heard her stop on the landing and go on, and the straw post hung still on its rope with the frost of the night still grey along its binding. Somewhere out in the main room a sweeper was pushing a broom across the stone, a long dry sound that came and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-48.md:19:"Feeling it after is the first half." Brom sat. "Tomorrow you'll feel it before. Then it'll stop." He leaned his back against the wall. "Your foot's still loud. But it's loud on the right beat now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-44.md:69:The Stone did not see it. In the fourth exchange the Blade found a gap that came of the Stone's own slowness, not of anything Reydan had seen in her feet, and put the flat of her sword against his ribs. The narrow man at the wall made a mark. The woman at the table wrote standing. The room made the noise rooms made for a tidy finish, and Reydan did not make it with them. He noted the gap, and noted that the woman had not known about her own back foot, and that she had won anyway. That was worth knowing too, about a room: a fighter could win here while still carrying a fault she had not found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-39.md:45:On a card night the main floor turned over bout after bout from the first lamp to the last, and a dark hour on it was money lost that Dace would feel in his teeth for a week. Tonight it sat empty from three o'clock, swept and roped and chalked, with the north mark and the south mark fresh on the stone. Dace had men up ladders all afternoon putting lamps on the beams, more lamps than Cael had ever seen in the building. By dusk the low ceiling was one field of small flames from wall to wall and the old foundry stone under it shone like the bottom of a pan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-13.md:237:Cael went down the stair slowly, one palm on the cold wall. At the foot he stopped in the passage while the list went on reciting itself in his head, *assessment records to date, with assessors' names*, and he understood that tomorrow, for the first time, he would walk into a sitting knowing for certain that someone a long way off had already asked to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-51.md:101:Men on ladders had been at it since the middle of the afternoon, putting lamps on every beam and every hook and every nail that would bear one. When they were lit the low ceiling of the old foundry was a single field of small flames from wall to wall, and the black stone underneath shone back at it. Cael had seen it lit like that twice before: for Keth, and for the Brom bout before that. It was brighter tonight. Dace had found more lamps somewhere. There was no corner of the building where a man could have stood in shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-52.md:129:The stone was cold. He noticed that first, oddly, before anything else, the old foundry floor pressing its cold up through his shirt into his ribs as it had come up through his boots every night of the autumn. Above him the ceiling was one sheet of small flames from wall to wall, and he lay and looked at it. Somewhere a long way off, six hundred people had made one sound and were still making it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:179:Fifty feet by thirty, the room was floored in oak laid on bearers like the east hall's, and the boards answered his first step. Brass ran through the boards in a grid, finger-wide strips set flush a third of a pace apart, wall to wall in both directions. Nobody at the long table would need to stand to see where a foot had come down. He had grown up at Hesk's bench, watching an instrument-maker scribe a line on brass and check it three times before he cut, and he knew what it cost to set a true line over fifty feet, in any material at all. Whoever had laid this had done it by survey and not by eye. At the far end, where a careless setter's work always wandered, these ran true. The crossings were as sharp at the back wall as at his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:He knew it as he had known the south chalk at his heels in the first exchange. The wall of fire on his left was closing round toward his back. Ahead of him, a pace off, the fourth channel of the true assembly was forming, the one that would shut the last gap. He could feel its first point already lit on the far side, and its second about to bloom on the earth a pace in front of his right foot, the air leaning there, heavy, sure, half a breath ahead of the light. When that point bloomed the fourth channel would join to it, and the whole assembly would light round him in a single instant, with a sound like an indrawn breath. There would be nowhere he could stand, and she would not need to touch him, and Wray would say *halt*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:59:"The panel does want that," said Quenna. She did not blink. "Four sittings of a framework and a read against a partner told how hard to hit. A careful reader starts to wonder what the candidate does when nobody's told the partner anything. I'd rather the record answered that question while I'm the one holding the pen." She paused. "It's also true. Both are true. Most things worth doing here have two versions, and both of them true. It's the one rule of this building I'd write on a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-45.md:239:"No," he said. "Yours was a choosing. You had two ways and you couldn't pick, and picking faster fixed it, as near as anything does." He flexed his right hand, slowly, and watched the dull sheen go out of the skin. "Mine isn't a choosing. I know which way. I know where it's going before it's arrived. Mine's a waiting. Something in me has to finish holding it before it'll let me send it on." He looked at the floor. "You can't hurry a wait by deciding to. That's the difference. That's why it's a wall and not a habit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:213:She went out. Lira was asleep already in the next room, and now Karis was writing by her own lamp next door; he could hear the pencil through the wall. Cael turned back a few pages in the binder to the last entry he had made at Greyvane. It was the term's count, written at the long table on the last night, before the gate and Prynn and the index. Under it there was half a page left. He looked at it, and then wrote two lines at the foot of it, in the square hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:69:"No," said Bracken. He laid his pen down square to the desk's edge. "They are evidence. They are admissible. They are not a verdict. I would be grateful if nobody in your hearing confused those three words. Nor a standing with a rank. A standing is this season's chalk. A rank is what the registry has written on a certificate, and the registry does not read our wall." He folded his hands. "Next year this academy will contest the qualifying season for the Continental. The chancellor has said so in an open address; it is on the record, so I may repeat it. A squad will be chosen. The people choosing it will read this season's closing standings among a great deal else. A strong season will not put a name on that squad. A weak one will very probably keep a name off it." He picked his pen back up. "Your friend from the north stands near the foot of a column she plainly does not belong in. If she climbs, it will be noticed. If she does not, that will be noticed too. That is all I may tell you, and rather more than I ought."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:117:*Day sixty-three — third bell — lecture stair — second landing, wall side — whole stair, top to foot.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:55:The second hour went slowly. The window above the landing was grey with cloud, and for a long time there was no sound but the river, thin through the stone, and once a mouse in the wall, busy and small. A little after the third hour struck he heard, far off, the readers going home along the walk on the range's other side: a door, two voices, a laugh, then a single pair of slow steps, then nothing. Some while later the fire-watch's boots came along the foot of the range on the courtyard side and went away toward the wing. He could not see the bench from where he sat. He did not need to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:169:Ephram came off the wall with his weight forward and stopped two paces out. He sold a strike at the left shoulder, hips turning, the back foot pushing honestly into the oak, and Brom's read took it; Cael saw Brom's shoulders begin to settle into the turn. Ephram was already gone from it. He had pulled the strike back into his own centre before it reached the halfway mark, and stepped a hand to the right, and sold another at the same shoulder from the new angle. Brom's read took that too, because it was honest weight again, and Brom was a man who believed honest weight. The third sale got no turn at all. Brom held, and held, and the strike did not come, and in the instant he let himself believe it would not come at all, it came: full, clean, a flat hand laid along his ribs on the side he had just stopped guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:149:Karis came out to the wall that evening with her own log, which she had kept since the bridge in a narrow book with marbled boards: every change at the ferry and the road's foot that any of the four of them had seen, the hour, the faces where faces could be told apart, a tick for on the bell and a small cross for late. She laid it open on the coping between them and ran her finger down a month of columns. Four late changes in a month, all four by under ten minutes, all four with a reason somebody had seen: a pie, a cart in the road, a dog, and once, memorably, a man who had stopped to help an old woman with a basket of eels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:119:The fighters came out to their chalk. The Force girl was tall and narrow and very quick off the mark, and she had a habit Cael had logged in the first month. She drove her first strike of every exchange at the same height, chest-high, to find out at once whether the other fighter would give ground. The Stone fourth-year was square and slow, and he rooted. When he set his weight he went down into the oak like a post going into a hole, and for the length of a breath he could not be shifted by anything less than a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:123:The Force girl drove chest-high, and the Stone boy rooted and took it, and she slid off him and came back low, and touched him on the outside of the knee with two fingers as his weight was still going down, so lightly that it was very nearly not a touch at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:139:It was the Stone boy's. He let the Force girl come chest-high again, and rooted again, and this time when she slid off low he was not there. He had let his own root go a breath early and stepped across, and his open hand brushed her shoulder as she turned, for about as long as a blink. It was as light as hers had been. On the tiers the same people muttered again, the other way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:143:The third exchange he watched badly, and knew it while it was happening, and could not stop it. The Force girl came in at a new height, low, testing, and the Stone boy rooted early to meet it, and for three or four breaths there was a hard close scramble on the oak, all elbows and set weight. Somewhere in the middle of it, a third of Cael's attention went up and across to the north tier on its own, without asking him, to find out whether the man in grey was watching the scramble or watching something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:153:The fourth exchange began. The Force girl had to score now or lose the line, and she knew it, and she came off her chalk faster than she had all evening. She drove chest-high, and the Stone boy rooted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:He found the corner behind the window by touch: the angle where the landing's inner wall met the foot of the upper flight, where the grey light from the window could not reach. He folded himself down into it on the stone, back to the wall, knees up, and laid both hands flat on his knees. On the gallery he had learned that his hands, left to themselves, went looking for jobs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:5:There was paper on every flat surface in the room. It lay on the long table in squared stacks and on the window seat in fanned ones, and on the floor along one wall in a row of open boxes, each with a card propped against its side. Three of the registrar's clerks sat at the table with their sleeves pinned back, writing. A fourth stood at a slope desk by the window copying from a heavy volume, with a librarian at his elbow who checked each line as he finished it and pressed a small brass seal to the foot of the page. The room smelled of lamp oil and new ink, and nobody in it was talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:He locked it. Shirts back; large case home under the bed and hard against the wall; then his shoulder to the foot of the bed, and the bed walked back after it until the frame met plaster. His body did that in every room without asking him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:The noon courier came down past them at a working trot, a lean man on a grey, with the house's wallet strapped inside a case against the saddlebag. He touched his hat to Karis without slowing. They watched him take the switchbacks one after another, a rider and then a small rider and then a speck with a grey under it. At the road's foot he went by the two men in road-grey coats just as the other two came up the river street to meet them, unhurried, on the bell. None of the four so much as turned a head. Nobody ever looks at a courier; that is what couriers are for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:11:Nothing moved. That was all, and every bit of it was hard. A building full of frightened people, waiting for eleven strangers and a man with no stated business, did not want to stand still. It wanted to sweep its passages and re-ink its ledgers and move its awkward things out of sight. Cael saw the want everywhere. He saw a Stone instructor stop in the second quadrangle with a broom she had picked up from somewhere, and look at it, and look at the flagstones, and then lean the broom carefully back against the wall where she had found it and walk away with her hands in her pockets. He saw two clerks from the bursar's office arguing in low voices at the foot of the lecture stair about whether the lamp-oil ledger ought to be copied fair before the twelfth. Then one of them said *posted day*, and the other said *posted day*, and they went back up the stair together without copying anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:Then he came back from behind, and that was the easy part, which was the strange thing about it. Once the touch had been given, his feet were his own again. The reserve was a Stone who rooted and held and was very hard to move, and not at all hard to step round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:231:Stone Paths read the ground. They felt a fighter's steps arrive through the boards before the eyes had them, and they rooted, and they countered off the root. He had the hill, and a Stone on a hill is a fortress somebody has built on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:267:He had to turn uphill to find her. Turning uphill, rooted, took him the beat that his counters always took. She had already used that beat. Her palm went onto his ribs before his weight had finished moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:315:*She took his ground from him twice, the hill and then the wall. Take a Stone's footing away and what's left is a strong man waiting for something to push against.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:65:He had to. With that word beside it, the name had no place left among the Copper. He rubbed it out of the top of the column, letter by letter, and climbed down and moved the ladder along the wall, and climbed again, and chalked it at the foot of the Iron column, under Ephram and the rest, with the new word beside it. Down there, under a column of strangers, it looked small. The porter regarded it for a while with his head on one side. Then he climbed down and put the ladder over his shoulder and went away to do the Silver board, and nobody in the yard but Cael had watched him do any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:71:She came in by the north arch with her kit over her shoulder, on her way to the session she had on the oak at that hour, and she saw the board before she was halfway across. Cael watched her see it. She stopped. She looked at the empty place at the head of the Copper column where Lira's name had been all season, and then down the wall to the foot of the Iron column, and then at the pinned copy beside the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:11:Cael's place was the end of the bench by the wall. Nobody had ever said so. It had simply become his, in every room like this one, because he liked a wall behind him and a window where he could see it. He put his left leg up on a stool, because the calf still complained when it hung. Lira, without stopping her thumbs, nudged the oil tin along the hearth with her knee to make room for his foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:293:In the first exchange she found out how he rooted: all at once, with his weight going down into the stone like a post into a hole. She took her touch there, at the moment he settled, with a flat palm on his forearm and a small hard spark of heat at the contact that made him take the arm back and look at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:59:A narrow bed under the slope of the roof; a washstand with a cracked jug; one chair; and the table, scrubbed nearly white, standing a little out of true, so that a pencil set down on it began after a while to travel toward the wall. A folded card, pushed under the short leg, cured it. From the window he could see the length of the yard: the stable lamps coming on one after another, a boy sluicing the cobbles, an ostler walking a lame grey up and down to watch which foot it favoured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:145:He had been watching people meet the thing they did not expect for the whole of his working life. A Stone officer in a collapsing gallery who threw up a wall so thick he brought down the roof he was holding off. A Current girl at a qualifying sitting who answered a stumble by pouring out twice her measured strength and burned her own hands on it. A magistrate's guard on a quay at night who met a stranger's sudden movement with a blow that would have stopped a horse, and the stranger only reaching for his papers. Every tier, every service, every age. They gave it more. Surprise came at them like a wall, and they hit the wall harder. It was the steadiest thing he knew about people under strain, steadier than anything in any manual, and he had never set it down anywhere, because something that every man does tells you nothing about any one man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:"It won't work on him. Hand him a true sentence with a corner cut off and he'll see the cut. He won't mention it. He'll simply know. Then the finest reader the Compact has will be holding a map of my walls." He turned his hands over. "A flat no only tells him that there's something I won't say, which he knows already, as does the whole bluff. A cut corner points his eye at the cut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:216:The other three, Cael saw, did not. Brom had seen the coat at the far end of the quadrangle, long before it reached the wall; his pebble had paused in his fingers for one turn and then gone on. Karis's head never came up from her page; so she, Cael knew, had spotted him earliest. Lira had turned her head, once, the length of the wall, the way anyone turns at a footstep; then she had turned it back to the river and kept it there. Of the four of them, she had spent the year wanting most to haul things into the open where she could hit them, and she let this one stand where it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:123:The sun had climbed. The bar from the gable slot, the high narrow bright one, had moved with it. It had come down off the far wall onto the floor, and it lay now across the middle of the boards on its long diagonal, from the north-east toward the south-west, a hand's breadth wide and painfully bright. As Cael watched, the yard-master took one of his short steps forward, and his leading foot came down in it, and the light went up his body to his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:81:That was all it ever was. It was not taking. It was nothing like closing a hand on something that was somebody else's. It was holding still in front of a thing, all of him, open, as you hold still in front of a bird so that it will stay. He held still in front of four exchanges of storm. And the four exchanges came together. The creases he had heard from the inside, the lanes he had stood at the ends of, the fire he had carried down his own left side, which he understood in that half-second was the part he had paid most for and knew best. All of it came together at once, without any hurry. It went into place like a cut stone dropped into the gap in a wall that has been waiting for it, and then it was simply there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:77:It was a new door in an old wall, and the stone had been cut round it so lately that the dust still lay white on the sill. A registry officer stood at the ladder's foot with a ruled board under his arm and watched the second hinge go on as if he would be blamed personally if it went on crooked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:At the inn under the outer wall he had read the row's report of it by a poor lamp, while the post-horses were led round to the yard below. It was a competent report, in Ilsev's office's careful even hand, and it gave him the exchanges in order and the figures, thirty and twenty-seven, and the duration, and a note on the crowd. Near the end there was one line that was not like the others. *Subject's footwork in the third exchange not previously recorded.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:201:He stood with his eyes shut after that, with the rain on his face, holding the place where the leaning was. It was not easy to hold. It was like holding a very small sound in a noisy room; he had to put everything else down to keep it. The harbour went. The gulls went. The wall under his feet went, nearly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:277:He took the residence stair one step at a time on Seventh-day morning and went down to the north wall to let the sun get into it. It was a rest day. The house was mostly asleep, or pretending, and the Crown yard lay empty under the long wall, where the porter's boards stood chalked fresh with the year's names. Down in the valley the two coats stood at the foot of the road and the two at the landing, and none of them looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:133:The intake had found the counter. A Stone first-year stood in front of Cael holding his form in both hands as if it might go off. Ahead of the Stone, two Current girls argued in whispers about a drill neither of them had run yet, and ahead of them a Mire third-year studied the notice board with great attention so that nobody would think he was waiting for anything. Cael had a floor-allocation sheet in his own hand, because evaluation-linked hours were lodged here or nowhere, and he spent the wait reading the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:179:By the sixth bell the man from the broadsides had copied it into his notebook at the gate lodge, and was riding down the hill with it as fast as a hired horse would take him. Cael watched him go from the north wall. The two coats at the foot of the road watched him go too. It was the first time in a week that either of them had turned his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:129:Cael knew the Force a little. A broad-backed young man who laughed when he was surprised; Rank Seven on the porter's wall since the last sitting of the old year, six ranks above Lira's one, and the best fighter the cohort had made in a straight line, without a program, since Ephram. A Force fighter's Path was a shove, and it went on contact. This one put it into the floor through his front foot. He stamped, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, and went on jumping for a count or two before they settled. Then the shove came back up into him, the way it always came back into a Force fighter along the line it had gone out by, and he had to take it in his knees before he could stamp again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:227:It was short. Cael could see that much from forty feet, from the way the pencil moved: four or five words, and then, after a pause in which Ephram looked out of the high window at nothing, two or three more underneath. He did not hide it; he simply wrote it facing the wall. When he had done he read it over once, and his face, which was a good plain open face that had never been much use for keeping things in, did something Cael had not seen it do before. It went still. It was the face of a man who has been adding a long column for two years and has reached the foot of it and written the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:Up there, pinned to the east hall door in Rooke's square capitals, was a card with her name on it for the program's first road meet. Rooke had crossed out its date a week ago and chalked another eight days later, when the confluence's stewards wrote that the meet was put back for repairs to their hall; she had minded for an afternoon and then been glad of the eight days. Up there was a red drill-book with a figure in it that was hers, and a broad patient man from the river academy who had written to ask whether he might come and teach her, after she beat him. Up there was a word she had never had before this year, and had to learn to say: *program*. Somebody had built a ladder, and leaned it against a wall, and put her name at the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:227:He went down the top stair behind Lira, at her pace, which was faster tonight than it had been. She went down standing now, one step at a time, with her hand on the wall and her right foot set down carefully but set down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:She had come out along the wall at the slow careful pace the ankle allowed, and sat down beside him on the coping by the third buttress with her right foot out straight along the stone. Below them the valley was going blue. The river had dropped since the flood and run back into its banks, and the ferry was working again at the landing, small and black on the grey water. At the foot of the road the two coats stood apart from each other as they always stood, and while Cael watched, the bell went in the lecture range and two more came up the road from the town and the first two walked down it. Nobody hurried, and nobody looked up at the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:237:The hall came to him as places where things were resting. The frames along the east wall stood on their feet on the boards, and each foot was a place, a small sure point where weight met wood and stayed. The lantern on its hook by the rail hung, and its hook in the beam was a point, and the point was asking. The bench Lira sat on had four legs, and the legs were four points in a square. The bar across the foot of the gallery stair was two points and a line. The great door had its hinges, three of them, all points, and along its edge, where it stood open against nothing, a long empty place that the new thing leaned toward with its whole attention, the way a dog leans toward a door with somebody behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:229:Cael watched it from the north wall, as he had watched it empty. The carts came up the switchbacks in the grey morning with the trunks swaying, and the ferry crossed full and went back empty, and at the foot of the road the coats stood against the chandler's wall and counted up what they had counted down. By the second bell the outer court was loud. By the third somebody was shouting for somebody from a window, and he could no longer hear the fountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:It was lit the way it was lit every night of the year, a yellow square at the top of the dark building, with the lamp set close to the glass so that you could see it from the north wall and the gate lodge and, Seln had once said, from the ferry landing at the foot of the bluff. The whole valley knew that window. A coat at the foot of the road could have told you the hour by it: lit at dusk, out somewhere between the tenth bell and the eleventh, never once dark before the ninth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:169:Cael heard it before he saw it, from the north wall: iron tyres on the switchbacks, and a horse that did not care for them. It was a two-wheeled cart with a deep bed and new spokes in one wheel, and the horse was a brown gelding with a white foot, thick in the leg and long in the tooth. Lira walked at his head with her hand on the cheek-strap, as she had been shown an hour ago, and talked to him steadily all the way up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:Cael stood at the end of the sound rock with the others and saw where a girl had been running three days ago. The shelf stopped as if it had been bitten. Beyond the break there was only the wall of the gorge, and along that wall a scar where the fallen stone had been rooted: a strip of raw pale rock four feet wide, tipped outward like the sill of a window, and heaped with broken plates. It ran on for thirty yards or so, and then the ledge began again, grey and level under its roof, as though nothing had happened. Under the scar there was a great deal of air, and he looked at that once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:They went out onto it together, the brown beast with her near flank brushing the wall and the square woman at her shoulder on the open side, a boot's length from the rim. The mule set each small hoof down and tried it. The woman's eyes were on the scratches; and where a marked stone lay under the animal's line she moved its head a little with her hand, and the foot came down there. At the far end Seln received the cheek-strap, and the boxes came off, and she brought the mule back as steadily as she had taken it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:Then there was nobody left on the near side but himself. He had known that it would be so, and had not let himself think of it. He set his left hand on the wall and his right foot on the first scratch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:115:"The ledge is whole for eighty yards from the bay," said Seln. "Beyond that it has gone, for thirty-one paces; I counted them, since I had to place a foot for each. What is left there is not a shelf. It is a lip of rock, where the shelf was rooted in the wall; four feet wide at the best of it, and tilted outward, and covered with what fell on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The house's farm lay on the back of the bluff, on the long south slope away from the river, where the wind was less. Below the stable passage were the kitchen gardens in their walls, and below the gardens the upper orchard and the lower, old trees in rough grass with their branches bare and black with wet. Below the lower orchard a stream came down out of a fold in the slope and ran along the bottom hedge toward the water meadows, loud over stones. The farm track went down beside it, and at the orchard's foot, where the track met the stream, stood a small stone cottage with smoke going up from it, which was the gardener's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:*Nobody has said it. Not since the wall, on the day after the case. Lira asked why we were still here and Karis priced it, and since then we have gone to every sitting and landed on the right foot and held loads and tied knots, and nobody has once said what any of it is for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:93:He took the third box after it, in the same manner, while the light went yellow on the far wall. Lira carried the stores, two steps and a slide, and would not be told that a bound ankle had no business on loose plates. By the last of her journeys the cart stood at the foot of the fan with nothing in it but its own seat board, and looked like a thing that had been robbed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:73:It was just as Seln had said. The wall stood over them, twice Brom's height in the middle, and at its foot there was bare clean floor, twelve feet deep. On the far side of it the shale lay banked to a man's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:The rock was a gift. It was dry under its roof, and rimed along the outer half where the roof stopped, so she kept in, close under the wall, as she had promised a fire that she would. Her left foot struck and her right came after it. She had the count in her legs. Forty. Fifty. The gorge went by on her right hand, blue and enormous, and she gave it none of her attention; she was reading the shelf five yards ahead of her toes, for cracks and for colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:201:She asked him that night, on the north wall, with the valley dark below and the coats' two lanterns small and yellow at the foot of the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:27:Lira came out along the wall while the last carts were still on the upper switchback, walking at the careful pace she had kept all week, and sat down on the coping beside him with her right foot out along the stone. She had come up from the town on the program's market cart with the tape and the chalk, three hours before him, and she had plainly been waiting to see him come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"The same quiet. Read again." He closed the book and gave it back to the lodge-man through his window with a word of thanks. Then he walked with Cael out under the arch and along the outer wall, where the wind came up the bluff off the river, and spoke to the wall rather than to him. "They have not gone. They have stood down. There is a difference. A man who has gone has decided he was wrong. A man who has stood down has decided he does not need to do anything, because what he wants is coming to him by itself." He walked a little way. "The corridor is on schedule. Every sitting sits. Every form is filed. Nobody on this hill has put a foot wrong, and so nobody has to come up the road and make one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:125:Cael got up from the wall, and his legs were stiff. He went across to the foot of the stair, to be nearer the light, and found that Lira had turned on her step to meet him, six above the floor, and was sitting with her chin on the stock of the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:9:It was a hard white morning, with the breath standing in front of every mouth, and the geese had not yet left the lee of the wall. The steading's woman stood on the step with her arms folded and a ladle in one fist. Lira was below her on the frozen ground, with her foot set flat and her chin up, and Karis stood a little behind with the crew's purse held in both hands, like a second at a bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:29:The floor was a ledge. It went round the foot of the four walls, three or four strides broad, under a foot of water; and in the middle of the room there was no floor at all. The plates had gone in there, a long time ago. He saw a hole as wide as a cart is long, with green depth in it. The water over it stood up in a low smooth dome and slid away on every side, and that was the spring. In the very middle of the hole one great slab lay aslant, like a table that has lost two legs, with its upper corner a hand under the surface.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:47:Cael was two strides behind him. He heard the knocking stop. It had been coming round the ledge after them, plate by plate, under the northern wall; and it left off between one plate and the next, as a footstep leaves off outside a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:He had a pebble of Lira's. He dropped it from the eastern wall onto the ledge below, where it lay under a foot of water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:127:Brom had got himself up to sitting. He sat astride the low wall as a man sits a horse, with his bound arm against his chest, and he was studying the gap beside him. Cael could read what was in his face. The gap was two strides wide. On its far side the wall began again, low, and ran on to the corner, and round, to where the others were; and at the foot of the gap, between the two ends of wall, lay the sill. It was the old work. A man who stood on it stood on nothing that the thing below could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:81:She had been set on an upturned trough by the barn wall, with her foot up on a sack. The woman had put her there on the first morning as she might have put down a bucket, and Lira had endured it for an hour. Then she had asked for a whetstone, and after the whetstone, for every blade in the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:207:"Oldest to the right," she said. "This first is a district hall's, and I had it off the step myself; it was dressed in my grandmother's time. The pale one is from a library that I used to be allowed into, and the honey-coloured is Ostrand's wall. And that is the oldest dressed stone between Denvash and the Line that anybody can put a year to. A mason was re-laying a drain at the foot of the old gate, the one that the city counts its years from. I call it the founding stone, and I have never owned anything older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:21:It took an hour. When it was done, the strips lay on the clay like the spokes of a wheel, eleven of them, each in the place of its own stone, with their heads toward the keg and their feet toward the walls. Beside every one stood a peeled wand, cut by Seln to her measure and set upright in a lump of well-clay. They had used a thumb's length for a foot. The tallest wand stood as high as Brom's shin, and the shortest was hardly more than a peg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:215:Cael looked, and it took him a moment. The walls of the station were the grey rock of the upland, split and laid in courses, and the lintel was grey too. But the sigil was not cut in the lintel. It was cut in a separate block, darker, closer in the grain, a foot square, and the lintel had been notched to take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:237:The track ran between the house and the frame-house, and a sill of stone was set in the ground there from wall to wall, with a socket at either end. There had been a bar across it once, and a keeper to lift the bar. It was the last place on that road where an officer could have put out his hand for a paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:39:"Gone," she would say, under her breath. "Back. Gone. It's like a wall that you can lean your foot on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:87:Cael paced the room from the door to the western wall, and then across. It was ten strides each way, as he had put it the day before, and that much of his count held. The roof did not. He had called it a foot above Brom's head; and Brom put up his left hand, and his palm lay on the grey with the elbow still bent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:251:Karis worked along the walls on her knees, and then standing, and then on Brom's bent back, which he offered without being asked and would not hear refused. She did not rub everything; she went down each course with her lamp held close and her lips moving, and wherever a group of strokes stood that she had not yet got on paper, she put out her hand behind her. Twice she left the paper altogether, and walked the foot of a wall with the short cord in her fist, counting the divisions within one line against the cord's scale rather than the distance along the stone, and wrote a figure where nobody could see it.

