   203	And there was what a round of three weeks had cost, which was the sum that all five of them would have been doing since a fork. It was not a kindness to tell them. It was a finding, and she gave people their findings.
   204	
   205	That left what she wanted, and she looked squarely at it, as she had on a road out of Oxhollow and under a cold stone. There was a calfskin with a strip of brass on it that pointed somewhere. Wherever it pointed, there would be an edge; and at the edge, somebody ought to be standing on the right side with her sleeves pushed up. She had said it in a loft, and she had been shown twice since what it was worth. And he was on the route, which meant one thing and no more.
   206	
   207	She went over what she was leaving out, as she would have gone over a bench at the end of a day, to be sure that nobody had been missed.
   208	
   209	There would be nothing in it about a plank. That stood where she had put it, and a strip of bark was no place to move it from. There would be nothing about a still thing under a boy's breastbone; she had told him to leave it alone, and she would not be the one who put a finger on it from two days off. She would not ask after a mule. And she would not tell five people that she had been glad of their bowl, since she had eaten out of it standing, on purpose, for nine days, and they had every one of them seen her do it.
   210	
   211	She dipped the pen. She wrote slowly, in a hand that had not been used since a healing house's morning list, and she did not write one word that she would not have said aloud at a gate.
   212	
   213	It came to four lines, and a letter for her name.
   214	
   215	*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
   216	
   217	---
   218	
   219	The carter came over the saddle on the hundred and thirty-first morning, with a hand's depth of snow on the road and his eyebrows white, and he had a strip of bark for Pike and another that was not for Pike at all.
   220	
   221	"From the healer," he said, at the inn's door. "She was writing. I never saw her write. I'm to say that it's for the five of you and that there's no answer wanted."
   222	
   223	Cael took it to the hearth. There were not a dozen people in that long room so early. Brom was laying out the bowls at their end of it, as he had at every halt, and Lira sat on the hearthstone with her right hand full of kindling. Karis had come out of the room behind the chimney with the hide under her arm, and Seln stood at the far end of the fire, by the small window that looked down on the landing.
   224	
   225	It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
   226	
   227	Brom had six bowls in a row on the board. He looked along them. He had put the last one down a little apart, as he always did; and he did not take it back.
   228	
   229	"Six, by letter."
   230	
   231	It was Lira who said it. She had turned on the hearthstone, and her eyes had gone past him to that window, where the snow was lying a hand deep on the sill. Then they came down to the grey wool across her own breast, and to a knot that was not hers.
   232	
   233	"She'll say when," she said.
   234	
   235	"After the thaw," said Brom.
   236	
   237	Karis had the hide unrolled on the board by then, between the bowls, with a pebble on each corner. There were four rings on it. The fourth lay at the very edge of the skin, where the carter had never had a wheel; and through the middles of the other three a line was ruled in pencil, very fine, which ran on through the fourth and off the hide.
   238	
   239	She laid the Ternhall brass along that line, on the three centres that she had paced, and took her hands away.
   240	
   241	"Yours," she said. "From the other end."
   242	
   243	He had the strip from Denvash in his coat. It was cold from the back room, and it took the firelight all along one edge. He set it down on the skin beyond hers, with its end on the ring by the marsh, and slid it up until the two lay in one length; and he bent, and looked for daylight.
   244	
   245	There was none. The edge lay on the dot at the hall, and on the dot at the Stair, where hers met it; he moved it along, and it lay on the dot at the Fallow. It touched the three for him exactly as hers had touched them for her.
   246	
   247	*The sites were on an alignment. Not random.*
   248	
   249	He did not say it. It was Karis who spoke, with her finger on the place where the pencil left the skin.
   250	
   251	"Someone made this pattern."
   252	
   253	She had not written it. She said it once, to the board, in the small exact voice; and nobody at that fire answered her. Cael counted four seconds by the tick of the wood. Lira put a stick on. Brom was looking at the sixth bowl.
   254	
   255	"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
   256	
   257	Lira, one-armed, feeding the fire: "Then we follow it."
   258	
   259	Brom: "After the thaw."
   260	
   261	Karis, writing: "Noted."
   262	
   263	Down the fire, the man who used to file reports about him watched the snow on the ferry landing with evident professional approval and said nothing, which — from him — was home.
   264	
   265	Nothing out here had read him. At three floors older than any registry, every Path in the party had stopped at a line and his had not; a fourth had been paced from outside and not crossed. Nobody alive — least of all him — knew why. Someone had made the pattern. That was as far as anyone could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:207:*The conditions, as I have them. Sustained: twenty minutes at contact is longer than any bout I've stood. Stakes: I'm three days off the Ring and the nine are still settling; she's three mendings down and her route's late at the far end and she's spending a fourth on a patient who's given her nothing four times. Earnest: she'll do the thing she does as well as she does it. Karis: the model says real, not fight. I don't know if the model's right. Nobody does. Tomorrow's the first time it could be tested and I'm the test.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:145:"Your arm on a floor again, a fortnight old. Four of you with nothing, inside a line, if it goes badly. And a healer who said not without her." He did not soften any of it. "So it's Millrace first, tomorrow, and she hears where we'll be. I'll not ask her to come. But she isn't going to learn afterward that we went."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:"My crew's at Millrace in two days," Teague said. "If you're late back, we'll come look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:51:"Find me at Thornwater," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:"Bring the map," said Oryn, to the road in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:That column had been growing more careful since morning. On the first afternoon every line of it had said a plain *yes*, and now each *yes* dragged a condition after it, like a cart with a pole lashed under its corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:25:*Seventy-sixth day. Forty-seven lines. Every one says yes, and most of them on a condition. The herd is over us still.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:73:He had not sent for her. She had come of her own motion, as she did every year. She went up the north water when the roads dried, to her daughter's hold, and set whatever that valley broke until the first hard frost. Then she walked down to Lowmarch, and asked a one-armed man where the crew was. This year he had told her Millrace. She had ridden down on the mill's cart, on the sacks, and had been sitting on the flat stone by the ford when Teague came off the ridge with a stake still to set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:1:# Chapter 47 — Millrace
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:123:*Hundred and thirteenth day. Millrace, the healer's bench, a little after noon. Asked her leave to touch the boy's wrist while she held it, so that I could learn what came to her hand. She said no. I stepped back. Entered before anything else today, since it is the first refusal under the rule and I want it to head the leaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"Ours. She came in at Millrace last night, down the mill water off the hill track, with a rope on her shoulder and no beast. It's the next gate on her round after that grain hold of yours, and she's sixteen days behind at it." He jerked his chin at the canvas. "I'm the mill's carter. I left a bench full when I pulled out this morning, and my own sister's boy on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:77:"Millrace," said Karis. "I have it on the hide: a square with a dot, a day down the south road from this town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:"South. Past the well you cleared, and up onto the ridge. It's a carter's day to Millrace by it, and the road forks above the mill." Pike rubbed the stump of his arm through the pinned sleeve. "One branch drops to the ford. The other keeps the ridge half a day more, and comes off the end of it, and the carts go round what's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:263:*Millrace. Three stakes of the weir, gone in the last high water. To be set before the ground shuts. Four marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had told a boy at a door that his crew would be at Millrace in two days, and it had been true when he said it only because he had decided, between one sentence and the next, to make it true. A man does not walk up to another crew and hand it the hair on his own neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:175:"Nothing declared," said Karis quickly. "By anyone. That's the hold, and it is my condition as well; I want to know what is felt, and I can't know that if somebody is doing something at the time." She was looking along the old line. "We go abreast, an arm's length apart, at a slow walk, so that we all come to it together. If you feel a thing, put your hand up, and say what it is like. Don't say anything before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:*Empty: sigil present, reserve gone.* She wrote it large, by feel. *Here: reserve present, sigil gone.* She turned the leaf. *These are not the same condition and must not be entered under one word.*

