editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:27:"That's three," said Brom. He had come from the tub with a wet bowl in either hand. "He said three, and there were three. I like a man whose count comes out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:41:Oryn regarded him. She had her bowl in one hand still, and with the other she had begun, by old habit, to push a sleeve up; she looked down at that hand and stopped it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:65:"It's why I asked you. A hundred and sixty-three days ago six people stood on a rim, over a place called the Stair, with crossbows. They'd cloths across their faces. No seals, no word, nothing you could have written down. One of them put a bolt in him. They knew which hole we were in before we'd climbed out of it." She leaned across the board. "You say nothing of yours crosses the Line. Something did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:77:"Four names," said Seln. "I showed them how it goes, six days ago, with bowls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:5:He stood just within the post, on the hinge side, where a person coming in out of the light would be three steps into the room before he saw that anybody was there. The long board lay beyond him, by the hearth. Brom had six bowls on it and the ladle in the pot, and the stool stood at its end with nobody on it. Karis sat with her notebook shut under her hand. Oryn was on her feet beside the stool, and Lira on hers at the hearthstone, with her back to the fire and her arms folded, the right over the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:27:Vastin came in. He made his bow, the short stiff one, to the room and to nobody in it; and then he stood at the board's foot with his stick, and Cael understood that a man who had been shown to a chair in every room of his life did not know which one was his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:31:He was looking at the stool. It had a bowl before it, and steam going up from the bowl, and nobody near it but a woman who was plainly not sitting down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:35:He said it with perfect gravity, and nobody at the board laughed. Vastin considered the stool, and the bowl, and the healer; he gave the matter the attention that he would have given a point of procedure which he had not met before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:41:He sat. It took him some time, with the leg, and he did it without putting a hand on anybody's shoulder. The keeper had come to his cask-room door to look at a stranger at the standing crew's board; Vastin lifted a finger to him, and asked what a bowl was, and put a copper down on the wood before Brom had lifted the ladle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:Brom filled the bowl, and set it before him, and looked at the copper. It was a thing that he could weigh, and he seemed to find the weight correct.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:75:He said it as he said everything, once, and without weight. Vastin bowed his head to it, by a little, as he had bowed to the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:83:He ate first. He ate as he had eaten the bread, neatly and to the end, and when the bowl was empty he laid the spoon across it and put both hands on the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:199:Nobody started, since they were not people who started. But Karis's right hand, which had been lying on her notebook, had gone flat upon it as if the leaf might blow away; Seln's eyes had left the yard, and Lira's fingers, on the board, had drawn in. Brom's two hands had stopped on the board. And at the board's end a healer who had swallowed a sentence nine days ago, at this same table, when a station was spoken of, stood with her bowl in both hands and looked at nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:247:There was a spoon there, in an empty bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:253:Brom had got to his feet, since the bowls were empty and he could not sit over empty bowls. He gathered them along the board in a stack, the six and the seventh; and when he came to the bench's end, Vastin's two hands lifted, and half rose toward the stack, and were too late. Brom had it against his chest already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:259:It was not said unkindly. Brom carried the bowls to the keeper's tub, and Vastin's hands went down again upon the wood, and lay there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:273:"That's four and a half since the boat," said Brom, from the tub, to nobody. He had his sleeve rolled to the elbow and a bowl in the water. "The fare, and the loaf, and the pot, and the straw. I've not spent four and a half since the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:She had Seln pace it along the outside of the standing fence. From the yard gate to the nearest grey back it came to three hundred and fifty yards, which was the distance at which she had once lit a fire under a ledge and seen nothing stir. Brom and the mule dragged the factor's hay sledge up the hill to the last of Teague's posts, and Lira sat on it with the bow across her knees. She had seventeen heads before her there, a hundred and fifty yards off, and the whole of the slope beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:103:She had worked that out from a sledge, with one arm tied and a bow that she could not bend, while four people with two good hands apiece stood round a page; he had been reading the ground, and she had been reading the only thing on that hill that could kill them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:199:He could not tell Brom to lift harder, since no lifting would shift a stone that was wedged. He went down on his knees in the snow, under the beak, and put both arms into the hole to the elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:The right-hand end came first, low, at his knee. He took it on his stave and felt it through his elbow. The other end was already coming, over the top, for his left shoulder, and he got back out of its way. Then it was the right again, and the left, and he went round the square before her like a man backing away from a mill. She was quicker than he was, as she had always been. Her feet in that great boot found the ground under the frost as though they had laid it themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:It was a down-stroke, from his right shoulder at hers, such as he would have given Teague's bow or the boy. She did not slip it. She brought the stave up level in both hands and took him square, on the bare wood between her two fists, and the shock of it ran up his own arm to the teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:235:A boy came along the carts a little afterward, with a flat cake in his hand, and laid it on the stump at Vastin's elbow, and went away again without a word. It was warm. Vastin had been waited upon at tables for the whole of his service, by people whose names he had taken care to learn, and he had thanked every one of them. He looked along the carts for somebody to thank, and nobody was looking back. So he ate the cake, and put a copper on the stump where it had lain. The copper was there in the morning, with the frost on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:243:There was a man on that landing who was hiring. He was brisk and well-kept, with ink on his cuffs and a clerk at his elbow, and he went along the waiting carters and the men without carts and looked at each as he would have looked at a bale, for the mark. He came to Vastin last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:5:"I shall not read six years by one lamp," she said. "A person who reads tired reads what she is hoping for. I shall count the sheets in daylight, on a table that is not full of elbows, and until then I shall say only what I have been handed. Six rows, in their tapes. What lies under them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:195:"You have had one bowl and half a loaf since yesterday. You held a boat this morning. You have stood at this table for the whole of a lamp, since you would not sit, and before that you sat on a stone trough for five hours with nothing to do, which I think was the hardest of them. You have handed over three things in the order that you meant to. I have them. I shall have them in the morning." She did not raise her voice. "There is nothing more for you to do tonight. You are to go up to your bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:209:He stood a moment longer. Then he took his arm, very politely, out of Brom's hand, and found his stick against the table's leg, and made the short bow. He made it to Karis; and then, since he was a just man, he made a shorter one toward the hinges.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:He did it again, with her walking at his elbow and her eyes on his fingers. He drew the cord exactly clear of the snow and not a hair beyond. She bent at the pebble, and at the peg, and at each knot as he came to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:One of the ten had sent its bill to somebody else. On the hundred and eighty-first day he took Lira's long knife up to the smith, with a copper of his own, and brought it back with an edge on it, and laid it beside her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:Teague's three had not spoken, and their captain was not even among them. He stood by the bench with his thumbs in his belt. He had looked at a place on the trodden snow, once, before the word was given. When it was given the spear was standing in that place, and the bow was where the spear had been, and the boy had the sack over the pegs and was waiting to be told whether he might put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:289:Lira had been ready since before it was light. The bow was on her back, with its one bolt, and the sling was knotted high at her collar-bone as a healer had left it at a fork. She had not spoken of that arm since a strip of bark was read out at a hearth, and she did not speak of it now. She went up the saddle road first, on the whole of her foot, and the rest of them followed her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"Standing." Pike considered him in his double fashion, and seemed to find the question a proper one. "That's the hearth's end you're lying at, which is the keeper's floor and my money, and I'll not explain that twice in one day. It's the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; he'll charge you for a second and he'll be right. And it's your names said to anybody who comes asking after a crew, which nobody will, the river being shut. That's three things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:305:Karis did not look up. It was Lira who answered him, from the doorway, where she stood on the whole of her foot with a bowl in her right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:9:Brom, who had six bowls laid along the board, set the ladle down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:"I said that my crew comes, and it comes. But I'll not walk it up to one of those places as it is. In there a Path's a thing that you had yesterday." He looked at the fire while he told it off, as he might have told off a load. "My spear's a tall body with a pole. My bow's a man with a bad hip. The boy's a boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:Nobody at that fire could dispute it, and Cael went over the offer as he went over any slip. Inside a line the other four were exactly what this man had said of his own; they had fought there as such, three times, with a bill and a knife and a borrowed bow, and two of them sat here bound up in consequence. He himself was another matter, which Teague did not know. But he had never once stood up against anybody with the ten fragments shut, and holding a thing down on a road was not the same as going without it in a fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:29:"Three touches. A touch is what the one who took it calls, since I've nobody to judge. Over the pegs is a touch." Teague drew one thumb out of his belt. "And nobody strikes what a healer's had her hands on this season. That's her arm, and his shoulder, and my bow's hip. And your left side. I saw the tape on you at a river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:He tried the outside. He went round to the tall one's left at a fighter's shuffle, to make the pole come across its owner's body; and the tall one turned on the back heel, a hand's breadth, with no more effort than a door takes, so that the rag was pointing at his breastbone again. He ducked in under it. It drew back through the leading hand like a bolt through a staple, a yard, and came out, and struck him on the point of the right shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:"Ten days, and you'll have that again. Bow, you're with the big one's bill. He's not to swing it; you're to show me how a man gets by a hook."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"Then you've a spear, and a bow, and a boy with a back like a young ox, and three iron bars. And there's a herd lying up over that ledge, by what the boy saw. Shift it as a carter would." She began to lay the webbing. "I've no Path that knits. I've told you so every year, and every year you've thought I'd find one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:205:The bow laughed, from the settle, where he had his bad leg stretched to the heat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:The cold road paid. He had salt work from now to the thaw, and more of it if the pass stayed shut. The bearing paid nothing; the bow had said so twice, and had been right both times. But Teague had told fifty people at a hearth that his crew would come, and he had not said it for a price, and he would have held to it if he had said it to a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:257:He lay in the straw until he had his wind. He had the limit now, and it was a plain one: the load went through him into whatever he stood on, and the ground had to be worth sending it to. The bill had begun already. His right forearm ached to the elbow, dully, like a tooth, and he knew that by morning it would not care to lift a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:23:Brom shifted along the bench to give her elbow room. At the far end the old woman turned her head, and regarded a boy who was being taught to wait by somebody else's crew; and she turned it back again, and said nothing whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The second time he did not wait to be sure. The rag came straight for his breastbone, and he let it have the place where he had been, by a hand's breadth, so that seven feet of ash went by his left shoulder close enough to stir his hair. *Out.* He felt it stop at the end of two long arms. *Home.* It began to gather; and he went in down the outside of the wood, with his sleeve rubbing along it, one stride and a second, until the leading hand was behind his elbow and the whole pole was a thing that lay beside him and pointed at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:59:He said it as he would have read a line off a slip, and he was already looking at his bow. But the tall one had not yet gone back to the eastern pegs. That long face was turned toward the bench, where a girl with her arm in a sling had her right hand flat on her knee; and it was in no hurry to turn away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:141:Brom got down on the ice like a tree being felled carefully. He had been told on a hillside what a man was worth who could not lift his right arm, and he had set forty-one posts since to find out whether it was so. He went forward now on his left elbow and his knees, with the hedging bill pushed before him, and the read told Cael what a big man lying flat did to a sheet: it spread him. The dish under him was three times as wide as it had been, and a third as deep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:He could see almost nothing by then. The snow a foot from his face was a white blur; he could not have counted his own fingers. He had never held the read for half so long, or leaned on it so hard, and it had taken his near sight from him piece by piece as the price. He went on backward on his elbows with his eyes shut, since they were of no use open, and called the ground to a man whom he could hear and could not see. *Left. Straight. Stand. Walk.* The boy sent each word up the bank, and up there a girl with one arm walked a mule round a drum and stopped her dead at every *stand*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:245:Lira had brought him the bowl herself, and she stood in the doorway while he wrote, turning her right hand over to look at the palm, which had a cheek-strap's print across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:5:The river had dropped a hand in the night, and it was still no river that Cael had ever seen at that place. It ran from his right hand to his left, out of the north, brown and loud, a good deal wider than the hundred yards that it kept in the dry months. The cable went over it from a post above the landing to its fellow on the western bank; and the weight of the flood had dragged the cable's middle so far down the stream that it hung in a long bow, with its belly well to the south of both posts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:7:The scow lay under the eastern end of it. She hung from that cable by two running blocks, one over her bow and one over her stern, and from each block a strap of plaited hide as long as a man came down to a stout post at her rail. Cael had crossed in her a good many times and had never looked at those straps. He looked at them now, since the ferryman was looking at them; the man went from the one to the other and hauled on each with his whole weight, and then stood chewing his lip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:11:He said it to Brom. The hauling rope lay along the planks in wet coils, as thick as a wrist. One end of it was made fast to the scow's bow. The rest went up the landing to an oak post at its head, where a block was lashed, and turned through that block into the round shed, to the drum. It was the rope that had drawn a cart out of the ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:47:Half the bank saw it and shouted. On a river a person might shout as he pleased, and it made no difference whatever. The ferryman had his sweep out over the northern rail, and he set its iron against the thing as it came down on him, and Cael saw him bend like a bow. The raft did not turn. It rode up against the scow's side and stood on its edge there, with the whole river behind it; her deck tilted toward the south, and eight people went sliding down it into the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:51:It was the strap. He saw the upper half of it fly up and hang from its block on the cable, twisting; and he saw the scow's stern, with nothing to hold it now, swing away down the river like a gate swinging on one hinge. She hung by her bow strap alone. The current had her whole length to push on, and all that kept her from going round that one hinge and tearing it out was the rope at her bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:105:Two beasts leaned. Teague's bow and the boy from the upper inn had their shoulders to the beam behind them, and three carters besides. The drum turned by a tooth, and held, and turned by another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:109:She lay almost end-on to him now, sixty yards out, with her bow toward the bank and her stern trailing down the stream. The ice had slid off her and gone. On her deck the ferryman was at the sweep, with his feet against the rail, heaving her head round. He could do that or he could do anything else. He could not leave it. The torn strap hung from its block over the place where her stern had been, with its tail whipping in the wind a yard out of anybody's reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:131:The scow's bow struck the landing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:149:He had let the read go when the bow struck. It had cost him as it had on the ice, piece by piece: first his own fingers, and then the block at his knee, and at the last the planks themselves, so that he stood at the head of a brown landing among shapes. He could see the western bank well enough. He could have counted the carts on it. But the people who came up from the boat were pale smears above dark ones, and he knew the woman only by the smaller smear that she carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:167:The brown shape counted something into the ferryman's palm. Then it did a thing that no carter on that river had ever done. It made a bow, short and stiff, from the waist, to the boat, or to the man who owned her, and straightened; and it stood on the planks with its hands at its sides and its weight laid evenly on both feet, the bad leg and the good, and looked at the place that it had come to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:261:He did not ask to come sooner, or to sit with them until then. He made the short bow again, to nobody, and went across the yard to the stone trough by the gate, and lowered himself onto its rim with his bad leg out before him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:The bar of Thornwater's eastern gate was drawn for them before they had called, and on the platform beside it a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:113:She had asked for that ankle once, from a mule's back, on a mountain road, and had been told that it was a tape and a season. In all the roads since then Cael had not heard her ask a living person for anything: not for a hand over a ledge, or a turn on a mule's back, or a bow spanned, though she had taken each of those when it was held out to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:123:She took her in to the plank. The door of the back room stood open, since that healer never shut a door on anything but a long reading, and from the well they could see a broad back, and a pair of elbows, and at the table's end a bare foot. They heard what she said first, as she said it to every person who lay down there: what she would do, and why, and what it would be like. It had knit as it lay in a river, crooked. She would have to open the way and hold it open, for the better part of an hour, and the girl would feel it going right, which was worse to feel than a thing going wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:161:She took the shirt out of the toe, a strip at a time. Then she put her mended foot into a boot that was two fingers too long for it, and stamped, and went across the yard to hear what a lean man on a platform had to say about a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:217:"Nobody did," said Brom, from the door. He was already counting bowls on his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:229:Brom laid six bowls. He looked along the row, and did not take one back. But Oryn would not sit on the bench with the five; she stood at the board's end with her bowl in her hand, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, where she could see the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:231:Cael counted the board, as he counted any room. There was a big man with one arm bound and six bowls laid; and a girl who kept turning her left hand over on the wood, as though it had come back to her from a mender's; and a small pale woman with a hide; and a quiet one with his back to the wall; and at the end, on her feet, a healer who had walked two days down a saddle because it was her road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:Karis waited until the bowls were empty. She had the hide on the board, spread upon Seln's oilcloth, with the brass laid along its pencil line, and a leaf beside it with eleven lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:245:She moved the finger to the kit's ledger, which lay shut at her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:263:It was Oryn whom he was watching. At the word *station* her head had come round, and her mouth had opened; he saw the sentence arrive in it, whole, as he had seen her sentences arrive since a mountain. She shut her mouth on it. She looked into her bowl, and turned the bowl a little in her fingers, and said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:281:Nobody else spoke. Brom gathered six bowls, and the fire ticked, and Cael sat on with her first two words going round in him. They were small words. Every road that the five had walked since the Line led away from them; and not one of the six had ever said the second of the two aloud as a place to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:5:She had sat all the cold term on a baulk of squared oak at the slip's head, with blocks under her bilges, and the blocks had been knocked out in the night. Now she rode on six rollers of peeled alder. The ferryman's boy ran round from her stern with each roller as it came free and laid it down again under her bow, and Brom walked backward down the slope with his good shoulder against her and his boots sliding in the tallow. Cael had a line from her rail, with two turns of it round a pile, and he gave it out a hand at a time when he was told to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:15:She went in with a slap, and sat down in the water, and swung to the length of his line with her blunt bow pointing up the river. The ferryman watched how she lay before he walked out along the landing, and made her fast at both ends with his own hands, and stood looking up at his cable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:57:It was a milking stool, black with age, with three legs of which no two were of a length, and he put the sixth bowl in front of it. He had laid that bowl a little apart from the others for three days, and for three days a healer had taken it up and eaten out of it on her feet; and it appeared that he had been thinking about that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:67:"It is." She took up the bowl, and stood beside the stool, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, and began to eat. "It'll do very well by itself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:75:He waited until the bowls were down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:79:Seln considered the board; then he reached out, and drew Brom's bowl into the middle of it, and turned it upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:81:"That is a gate. A man sits in it who is paid by the registry, and he has a book." He laid Karis's shut notebook beside the bowl. "He does not care who comes in, since he is not a guard. He asks each person for a name, and where the name is registered, and he writes down what he is told; and the person walks on into the town and buys bread. It is the mildest thing in the world; I have watched it done ten thousand times, and I never saw a hand laid on anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:"I stood in one at fourteen. It took eleven seconds over me, and wrote a word, and I've been that word ever since. I know what sits beside the frame now, and what it's for. If I stand in another, I come out as a column that somebody's chosen for me, with everything else written off. Or it goes dark again, and that's a second paper with a seal on it." He looked at the upturned bowl. "That's what I'm afraid of. It isn't the walls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:217:They were making ready for a road. The tall man sat on the mounting block with seven feet of ash across his knees; he had drawn off the wad of rag that it had worn all the cold term, and he was seating a leaf of steel on its end and driving the pin home with small exact blows. The bow had a new string, and was running a lump of wax along it with his bad leg stretched out, while the boy packed, and unpacked, a load that would have done for two of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:225:He did not try it on anything. Cael did not know what Path that man carried, since nobody on this bank had ever told him; he knew only that it lived in that wrist, and had been kept shut for a hundred and twenty-four days by an old woman's order. There was a post at Teague's elbow and a stone trough at his knee, and a boy would have struck one of them, to see. Teague looked at his own knuckles for a breath, and let the hand hang, and came down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:63:A thing given so cannot be shared. He would never know for certain that it had been read. He could not stand beside the boy in it, or be asked what he thought, or say that he was sorry. He could not be thanked, which he did not mind; he could not be blamed either, and he found, with some surprise, that he minded that. He would be handing over the heaviest paper that he had ever carried, and he would have to do it as a man slides a bowl along a board, and look away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:167:Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of an iron stake that lay in a tarred case, three feet from her elbow, with its point still bright. It was on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:15:Seven bowls went round, and the mule had the open part of the pen to herself, where she stood with her rump to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:Oryn ate standing, at her stride and a half, and when her bowl was empty she set it down on the wall's top and pushed neither sleeve up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:"You laid seven bowls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:29:"I've laid a bowl for you since a heap of stones. I never thought that it meant you'd go down a hole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:87:"You said a station, at that board. He said it first, by the fire, and then with a bowl turned over. I know stations. There's one on the crown of every city inside the Line, with steps going up to it and a square in front, and I've stood in such a square with a line of children and watched them go up one at a time." She did not push up her sleeves. "It's the one thing in a town that's built to read whatever is brought near it. And I've had my hands on you, a long while back, and I found a thing that I made you write down and leave alone. I've not touched it since. I don't know what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:137:"Then you'll put the thing down and leave it down, which is harder." She had gone on to the meal-sack already. "You, I can find nothing the matter with. You've not slept a night through since a ferry, and you'll go down under a town and forget that people eat. I can't mend that. Somebody's to put a bowl in front of her and stand there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:183:She took up her bowl from the wall's top, and looked into it, and found it empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:207:There was not a great deal else to be done, since Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them off a landing. Lira took the tarred case in her left hand, by its strap, and nobody remarked on which hand it was. The healer's pannier stayed on the mule, with her linen in it, and a bow hung beside it with its one bolt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:221:"It's six bowls," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:129:Vastin had not sat at all. There was room on the bench, since Brom had left it, and he stood by the door-post instead, with the stick and the bound leg, and ate what was brought him from a bowl held in his hand, as a man eats at a muster. Lira watched him do it for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:143:He had set his bowl on the sill. He spoke to the fire, and it was plain that he had been arranging the thing since the hound was lifted off his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:167:Vastin received it as he had received the price of a loaf. He bowed his head by a little, toward the voice, and it was not the short stiff bow; it was a good deal less practised than that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:177:Oryn had been standing by the hearth's end with her bowl. She watched the door until it had shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:179:"He took every word of that," she said. "About the month. He heard it, and he told me that he couldn't undertake it. And he's not knelt since, or gone faster than a walk, or leaned on anything but that stick." Her spoon went round the bowl. "I've had patients who said *yes* to everything and did none of it. I never had one before who said *I cannot undertake*, and then did it to the letter, as if it were a thing that he'd thought of himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:255:She looked at that slab as Cael had seen her look at a wall of cut courses in a marsh. It was off the road by a furlong; a person might speak there. It was in daylight, and dry. And there was not a bench near it, or a meal-sack, or anybody's elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:"I am packing," she said. "I said that I should count in daylight, on a table without elbows, and I have been awake since the middle of the night working out where such a table is to be had. It is not in this inn. So everything goes into the flat, in the order that he gave it, and the flat goes where I go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:79:It went up slowly, and a little crooked at the elbow, and it shook. But it was the first time since a white floor on the Fallow that she had voted with it, and she held it there until he had seen. Brom's left went up beside it. Karis shifted the flat, and raised her right hand with the glove still on. Seln lifted two fingers, and Cael put up his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:87:Nobody at the post said the name. They had laid a sixth bowl for it every night since a heap of stones, and had seen it eaten out of standing; and not one of the five would have named her to a ledger without her leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:115:Teague's salt was going down the planks. There were forty sacks of it in a grey heap at the landing's head, and the ferryman would have no cart on his boat yet, or any bale that a man could not carry; and so it went aboard on backs. The bow took a sack at a time, and came up again easing his weight onto the right leg. The boy took two, and was told by nobody to take one. The tall man carried his on the shoulder that the spear did not use, with seven feet of ash in his other hand, shod with its steel again, and he set each sack down in the scow's waist square upon the last, as though he were laying a course of stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:145:Vastin had his copper ready. He gave it with the short stiff bow, and stepped over the rail bad leg first, and went aft without being told, to the post where a new strap of stern line had been rove. He stood there through the whole of the crossing with his hands on his stick, a foot from the thing that he had held two days before with his back, and did not touch it. The ferryman, at the sweep, looked once at him and once at the strap. Cael thought that it was the nearest that man had ever come to thanking anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:151:Brom had the purse in his fist before the mule was off the scow. He had carried that debt since before the snow: sixty yards of four-strand rope, to a ferryman who would take no silver for it. A brisk man with ink on his cuffs came along the stack to meet him, with a clerk at his elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:169:It was a road again as soon as the landing was out of hearing, and the hold came down on all of them as it had on every road in that country. The sigils stayed in their pockets. Cael's ten lay shut; the read asked at the first bend, civilly, what the wet ruts weighed, and was told that it need not know. Seln went ahead by thirty yards with his hands a little away from his sides. Lira came next, with the bow on her back. Brom had the mule's cheek-strap, with Karis at the off side of the load and Oryn at the near, and Cael walked behind the mule, as he always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:185:"Out here it gets you eaten. They come to it all together, from as far as they can feel it, and they don't leave off while it lasts. So nobody declares on a road. A carter who's Iron to the elbows will let his wheel go over a bank before he'll bring up the grey to hold it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:Brom had brought seven bowls out of the pannier, and nobody had seen him count. He filled the seventh with what there was, which was cold barley and a heel of cheese, and set it on a stone; and Vastin thanked him by name. A little afterward a copper lay on that stone beside the empty bowl. Brom looked at it for a while, and left it where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:17:There was a brown coat at his elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:189:They went over the worn place and down, running, as they had come up. Cael, who stood on that same bank forty feet lower, saw the whole of it. Fifty-five yards of scree went by under them like water. At the bottom lay the bowl, round and grey in its shadow, with the scrapes about its rim. They did not go to the scrapes. They went straight out onto the ice, the three abreast, to get the breadth of it between themselves and the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:75:The factor regarded Vastin, who made the short bow, and it appeared to answer the purpose, since the bar was drawn. The yard within was small and very clean, with a long low house on one side and a salting-shed on the other. The shed's door stood open on a row of empty hooks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:117:The hollow was round, like a bowl, and it lay in the spur's shadow, where the day never came. There was ice on it. The ice was grey and dull, with water standing on it at the edges, and Cael knew that grey: the shelf at Lowmarch had gone that colour a week before the river took it. At one side, under a lip of rock, there was a black place where it had not shut, and he could hear water going down there, a long way, into the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:119:Round that bowl's rim the stone had been scooped. There were hollows in it, each the length of a hound, worn as smooth as the seat of an old chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:191:Lira had unslung the bow. She held it out to the healer, with its hook and its one bolt, and it was the left hand that she held it in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:219:Karis stopped there, at the bank's end, with the mule's cheek-strap in one hand and Hesk's clock in the other. Oryn stood beside her with her arms bare to the elbow. Neither of them said anything, since there was nothing that the road allowed.

