    73	## New canon minted
    74	
    75	- **The slip and Pike's rule (flag).** The clearance slip came over in the bag on the 277th; Pike drew a road between two heaps beside it. Twenty, "at the hold's own gate, on the factor's thumb, when the factor's walked it". **Pike will not write a clearance taken until a healer is said** (his own rule since his second year, when one of a crew of four bled for two days on a hurdle). His sixth line: *And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.*, the first column he has had for Oryn.
    76	- **Oryn's round this thaw (flag).** She goes west with the six as the slip's healer; from the fork-stone north to "five gates up along the hill-foot", one with a child that she was to have heard a cold term ago; then "down the far water to the ford, and over, and on to the mill from above": her own round walked the wrong way about. Three weeks puts her at Millrace.
    77	- **Teague (flag).** The forty sacks went aboard on backs on the morning of the 278th; he is still on the east landing when the six cross, bound for the block on the cold road. He had first call on the slip by the cloth and left it. He has carried salt to the shoulder hold and led mules through its cut at noon with their heads held. "A man that holds a thing so doesn't leave off because nobody's asked him. I'd know what I had at my back." The word left with Pike: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever is beyond it; no day.
    78	- **The ferry and the rope (flag).** On the 278th she crossed in the forenoon with people, led beasts and carried sacks; no wheels. A copper a head, the mule a head. **The broker's cordage on the western landing is three-strand, hawser-laid. Brom refused it** ("A four's laid round a heart, and it keeps its round in a block, and a ferry's rope lives in blocks"). The sixty yards of four-strand are still owed.
    79	- **The border road.** One of two roads out of the western landing (the other runs south and west three hundred miles, to the ford and the station that Vastin crossed). It climbs through alders to a moor; a halting-ground in a fold by a beck, eleven miles out, with a dozen dead rings; a long shoulder of hill; the cut; four miles of high moor to a spring; six more to the fork-stone; a long rise of a mile and more.
    80	- **The four hands (flag).** Flat, palm down: *stand*. A shut fist: *seen*. Two fingers: *come on*. A hand turned over empty: *nothing*. The doctrine in one line: "Nobody declares unless they're meant to be loud. One of us, once, in a place that we've chosen beforehand."
    81	- **Stillhounds, added to Book 7's account (flag).** One paced the column for half a mile in the hour before sunrise ("dusk turned round") and stopped at a beck. **Karis's sixth line (16:117):** *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* The den at the cut had learned that whatever declares on that ice slides to the foot, and ran past the place of the declaring. Eight laired round the sink, three of them under the north bank where the lip hides four yards.
    82	- **The shoulder hold and its factor (flag).** A foot-track leaves the cart road at a cairn below the spur; a plain bar in a wall as thick as a cart; a small clean yard, a long low house, a salting-shed with empty hooks; no salt up since the first frost. The factor: spare, grey, a leather apron, a slate on a cord, a sheep-crook, pale eyes; a thumb-mark like a crook; **no name and no pronoun on the page**. The cut was made by "my grandfather's people". Three carts, a carter and two mules lost since the frost. "They never came up for a cart that kept its feet."
    83	- **The cut (flag; BOOK_MAP §8 extended).** A hundred strides, a cart and a man wide, rising east to west by the height of two men. South bank: thirty feet of scree at the pitch of a steep roof, with the hold's sheep-walk on its lip. North bank: low enough to step onto; beyond it fifty-five yards of scree down to the sink, a round bowl in shadow where water goes into the hill, iced grey and rotten, with a black unfrozen place under a lip of rock and scooped lairs round its rim. A seep at the cut's head runs down the middle of the road, a stride wide and a hand deep, under ice that never thaws in that shade. Three runs worn over the north bank in the upper half. Three wrecked carts at the foot.
    84	- **Iron Wall as shown (flag).** Named by Vastin ("It is called Iron Wall. It is on a roll, with a rank beside it"); **no tier is stated**. He steps into the road and brings the stick's foot down once; a sound "like a mallet on a cask" comes out of the road; a line runs from his boots across the cut and cracks the ice white, a hair wide; "the air stood up from it": a face from bank to bank as tall as the scree, seen only by the frost that lies on it. It holds eight hounds at a run without ringing or shaking. Limits said and shown: it does not go anywhere; **he may not lift a foot** ("if I shift so much as a heel, the whole of it is gone"); each blow on it comes down through him into the stiff leg; it goes out of him steadily, "the loudest thing that had been done on that road"; it shuts the road and not the banks. It falls when he lets it go. Oryn's two fingers: "You're at the floor." **"A Path of the tribunal floor… taught for fields; and a field is ground where a thing has been declared, a hearing, or a square, or a gate with a crowd at it." "I have stood on eleven such."** Karis has read of it in "a manual of engagement fields, with a diagram".
    85	- **The fight's count (flag).** Brom's rock at the plane's south end (one); Lira, two-handed, on the hound at Vastin's knee (two); one broken on the plane in the second run (three); Seln's knife on one standing in the runnel (four); Brom's rock on the other (five). Three over the north bank and through the sink's ice. Nothing came up.
    86	- **Bodies.** Vastin: four holes and a tear below the right knee, shut by Oryn; the joint "wrung like a cloth", bound with linen and two laths; **the bad knee is the right** (Movement 2 gave no side). Brom: the right boot torn from lacing to welt; two toes bruised, bound together, ten days.
    87	- **Oryn's past (flag; the earlier books say nothing).** "I was made in a city, inside the Line. It doesn't matter which." A house for the sick, forty beds, six healers; her Iron fourth rank is from it and is provisional because she "never stayed to have it made anything else". **A sheet on a board at each bed's foot: name, complaint, what had been spent, and a column for the patient's tier.** In a week of fever the spending went down the column from the top; a woman from the river sheds with no tier went to the bottom of the heap; Oryn went to her at night, off the sheet, and it was enough (**nobody died**); the woman who kept the house said that a healer who spends off the sheet has stolen from whoever stood next on it, "and I couldn't fault a line of them". She walked out of a gate in the coat she stood in and has not been through one since. Of stations: "There's one on the crown of every city inside the Line, with steps going up to it and a square in front."
    88	- **The spring (flag).** A furlong south of the road, four miles past the cut: an outcrop as big as a barn, a stone-built basin, and a fallen slab twelve feet by six, waist-high, out of the wind.
    89	- **The abstracts, read in daylight (flag).** Karis counted every row twice and had each counted again (Lira the first, Cael the second, Brom the third; the fourth set by for Seln; the sixth her own); **no figure for any row is printed**. **Five groups of signs, the same five in all six rows.** Four are the hide's four by their measures (three alike to the stride, one double). **The fifth is "larger again than the fourth"; not on the hide; its place unknown.** **Five columns:** the day; the station (eight points, by the compass and its halves); the measure; *Instruments*; and the anomaly. No radius of any place has changed by a stride in six years; four surveyors by their figures; three clerks' hands (round; cramped and leaning backward; capitals like gates). The fourth column reads *Cord true. Edge true. Level true.* on every sheet. The three oldest rows have a ruled space at the foot headed *Remarks*, empty but once: **the Stair's sheet in the fourth row, in a hand that Karis cannot tell from the dead surveyor's**, carries the protected remark, against the fifth column. From the next year's printing the space is gone.
    90	- **Seln's five omissions:** no routing head; no surveyor's name, a number in its place; nothing to say who ordered the survey; no column for a cause; the remarks space struck from the block after the year of the remark.
    91	- **Vastin on the form (flag).** "A survey abstract of the second schedule… the sheet that stands in place of the field book when the book has been called in. The book itself does not survive it." Drawn at the year's end in the office that has the charge of the stations. **That office keeps its own year, which opens later than the seat's; a date in the stations' count must be turned by taking off the difference, a fixed number of days (given aloud to Karis; not printed). A return that comes up from a station is dated so; a paper that goes down from the seat is dated the other way.** His two false starts: an order to re-tape each row under two hands, and "restricted matter… under the first article of the closed schedule".
    92	- **The box (flag).** To the right of the four signs and the number, on every abstract, a small printed square with a heading in the unread signs. **On all four of the hide's places it holds the short stroke and the bar, drawn alike; on the fifth it is "not filled as ours are"** (Karis will draw it and describe it to nobody). "It is a class." Nobody says who filled it or by what rule.
    93	- **The sum (flag).** The registry writes, on the first line of a person's file, after the name and before the bracketed word, the day on which the person was classed, in the seat's count; every paper served on Cael began with it. Seln copied that line twice when he was employed at the house above Ostrand. The technician's date, turned, is that day. **No figure and no span is on the page.** Seln took the sheet from the foot of the second row during the reading and did the turn three ways at the basin; he set it before Cael with its head away from him.
    94	- **The fork-stone and the fold (flag).** A slab as tall as Brom, a worn hand cut on each face. A round drovers' pen of dry stone a little down the northern branch, a quarter of it roofed with slabs, with a hearth and flue in the wall and a stack of peat: "You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go." A fire with a wall round it and a roof on it is not on a road.
    95	- **Seln on the provision (flag):** "A provision of that kind is granted by the week." Belief in the first; work in the second; nobody leaves at the end of a second.
    96	- **Treswick from the rise.** A green plain with hedged fields; a river out of the north in loops; a bluff in the nearest loop; three walls in steps (a long grey one at the foot, a second with slate roofs inside it, a third close and pale about the crown); a small pale steady light above the highest roofs. A day's walk off.
    97	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the shoulder hold's factor and "the hold's woman"; the broker and his clerk on the western landing; the keeper of the house for the sick, the Gold with the wrist and the woman from the river sheds in Oryn's account.
