editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:It had Hesk's book half out before he knew what it was about. He had written down everything that had ever happened to him. It was how an old man had taught him to keep from being told what he was: one noted the day, and the count, and the trend. He had the pencil. He had the place, under yesterday's line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:"There are five places," the old man said slowly. He was not speaking to anybody. "They have been measured for six years by an office that told no other office. They are classed, by a mark that the survey's own people carry on their iron. A surveyor who formed a view of them had the space for his view struck off the form, and is dead. And a return upon the deepest thing that the Compact owns has been laid among their sheets, for want of a shelf, with a brush drawn through it." His voice had quickened, and gone level. "This has to go up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:195:Vastin was silent. Cael watched him do the thing that he had been told, and it was a strange sight; the man had read rooms for forty years, in an order, giving each object its due, and he now had to look at one without doing anything to it. His right hand came down, and found the stick. His eyes went to the slab.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:93:It was no more than a length from Cael. It did not so much as turn its eye to him; he was a quiet body, and there was a loud one two feet beyond. It went by his knee like a grey wind and took the old man by the stiff leg, a little below the joint, and shut its teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:121:It was done in one stroke, close in, with the whole of her quick shoulder behind it, and it was not a thing to look at. The jaws came open. The hound went down across the old man's boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:209:Vastin shut his eyes. He did not do anything that Cael could see. The frost that had been lying on nothing fell, in a thin straight line, across the road and the broken ice. There was a hill with a cut through it, and cold air. Up on the scree a plate shifted and ran a yard, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"I've a thing to ask, and it's not about the money." The factor had not gone in. The pale eyes were on Vastin, who stood by the gatepost on his stick with the leg bound stiff. "I was on the lip. I saw eight of them run into the air and stop. I've had Paths through this gate for forty cold terms, and I never saw that, and I'd know what to call it if a carter asks me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:107:"It is called Iron Wall," said Vastin. "It is on a roll, with a rank beside it which would mean nothing on this road. I had not declared it in a great many years. I should be obliged if a carter were told that it was declared by a fool, in the wrong place, and that he was got out of it by five people who never made a sound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:33:Cael read the slip a second time, as he read anything, and it was the last five words that he read. Two roads left the western landing. One ran away to the south and west for three hundred miles, to a ford and a station and a house with a seal, and an old man had come down it on a stick. The other was the border road. It went up over a moor to the Line by the short way, and Cael knew what stood at the end of it, since a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth and told him; and last night he had read it again, in a square black copy, under a title.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:145:Vastin had his copper ready. He gave it with the short stiff bow, and stepped over the rail bad leg first, and went aft without being told, to the post where a new strap of stern line had been rove. He stood there through the whole of the crossing with his hands on his stick, a foot from the thing that he had held two days before with his back, and did not touch it. The ferryman, at the sweep, looked once at him and once at the strap. Cael thought that it was the nearest that man had ever come to thanking anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:203:"No. I heard it as I did it." The old man's face had not altered, but the back of his neck had gone a dull red above the frieze. "I have acknowledged every instruction that I was ever given, aloud, and I have required it of others. I beg your pardon. It will not be the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:209:The halting-ground lay in a fold of the moor beside a beck, with a dozen rings of stones on it, and black in every ring. No fire had been lit there since the first frost. The carters of that country ate cold on a road, and Cael had long since left off missing a flame at dusk. He saw Vastin look at the rings, and ask nothing, having been told why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:231:"May I ask a thing that I ought perhaps to have asked this morning?" Vastin had his bread in his hand, and he did not lower himself onto a stone until he had the question out. "You have told me what I am to do if one of them comes at me. You have not told me what I am to do if it comes at one of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:265:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:5:"That is the second coin which this road has declined of me," he said. "An old man with three carts left the first on a stump."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:33:The grey head swung, low, once to each side. It passed over a mule, and a small woman, and a large man bent at a girth. It passed over an old man at a ditch as it passed over the thorn beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:47:"I do not say it as a complaint. I am trying to enter it correctly." The old man was a little out of breath, and he had kept his place, a dozen strides back, so that he had to pitch the words forward. "I have not gone into a room in forty years that did not turn to see who had come. I have just stood within fifty yards of a thing that hunts, and it went over me as it went over a bush. I find that I have no column for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:209:"I shall be a stride behind him," said Vastin. "I can undertake where I shall stand. I have told you what I cannot undertake, and I had rather say it twice than have you find it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:They did not all sit. Vastin stood in the gap of the fold, where the hurdle went, with his back against the stone and the bound leg out before him. He had come six miles from the spring on that stick, at the tail of the column, and had been offered a mule twice; and he had the grey look of a man who is counting what is left in him and finding it sufficient by a very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:295:They went down off the rise in file: a quiet man in front, and a girl with a case in her left hand, and a big one with a box, and a small one with a hide. A boy of nineteen came after them, who had been taken to a great many registries in his life, and sent for by some, and filed in all of them. Last came an old man on a stick, who had been a marshal and was learning to be nobody. Cael had never before walked toward such a place because he had chosen to. He kept his eyes on the lowest wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:213:"Then you will understand me the more readily when I say that my trail stops there. I could not follow it. An archive on the Line keeps a book at its door, as a gate does, and every reader is written in it with the office that he comes from. My name stood in such books for forty years as the certifying office. If I had gone to that door with the seal, the request would have been minuted, and whatever shelf I had asked for would have been held by evening. If I went without it, I should be an old man of no standing, asking for the lowest shelf of a border archive, and I should not be let past the porter." The left hand opened a little, and shut. "I have never been able to go where that line points. I have only been able to carry it to somebody who might."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:235:He had both palms on the board again. The fire had burned down a little, and Cael's eyes had come far enough home to show him those hands plainly at last. They were an old man's, and a clerk's, and a strap of plaited hide had been across both of them that morning. The right was raw from the heel to the fingers' roots, and the left had fared no better, and had never opened flat since he sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:211:"I am looking for five people," said Vastin, "who came east by this road before the last cold. Where would a man ask after them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:221:The second was an old man with three carts, on a halting-ground beside the river, at dusk. There were rings of stones on that ground, and nobody had lit a fire in any of them. The old man came along his wheels while Vastin was lowering himself onto a stump, and looked him over without haste. He had pale eyes, with the skin about them folded very small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:233:"I know who they'd have had." The old man turned to go back to his mules. "Go to the plank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:29:On the far bank the carts had drawn up to the planks' edge in the half light, and Cael watched a long thin figure walk along them with one arm held out stiff, turning them back. A wheel went onto the landing and was made to come off it. Then the people went aboard, one at a time, and were placed. He counted them as they stepped down, since his far sight was as good as anybody's. There was a man with a ledger under his arm, and two with the walk of carters, and a woman who lifted a child in after her; there was a broad one in a drover's hat, and a lad. Last came an old man in a brown coat, who gave his stick to the lad to hold while he got his leg over the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:She came up the curve of the cable a yard at a time. It was nothing like nine minutes; Karis told him afterward that it was twenty-two by the clock, and that she had not known that she was counting aloud. A tree went by between the scow and the bank. The woman had her child's face pressed into her coat. The ferryman worked his sweep without once looking behind him, since a man who stands so on a strap does not need to be looked at. The two carters and the drover stood about the old man with their hands open, wanting to help him and finding nothing of him to take hold of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:139:He was standing over him with the stern line in his fist. The old man looked up, and then down at the strap across his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:"I had a tray for such papers," said Vastin. "I kept one on my desk for forty years, marked *held*, and I had supposed it a habit of my own. A paper in it is neither granted nor denied. It waits. I learned in this cold term that somebody else keeps such a tray, and that it is a great deal deeper than mine was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:41:"That is the first," said Vastin. "It is yours now. It is a great deal of paper to say that an office has been walking round your four places for six years, counting, and has told nobody. I should like the second laid apart from it. At the bare end, if I may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:53:"It is a copy," said Vastin. "I shall say so before I am asked, since she will ask. The line stands in a record that is older than the section it is filed in, in a room where I had the right to read and no right to copy. I copied it. I set down the word and the place exactly as they are written there, letter for letter, and what stands round them, so that nobody need take my account of where the line sits."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:173:*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:55:"Nothing that can cross the Line," said Vastin. "I shall be exact, since you ask it so. I broke no article of the code in leaving that I know of. If a query has been raised on me, it is a personnel matter, and it runs in a district. It wants a counter to be served at. It will have gone as far as the last station on the east road, where there is a counter, and a book, and nobody to open either. My old office never had a hand on this side of that sill. It would not know how to send one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:Vastin looked at the webbing for some time. It was the look that he had given a margin with a clerk's four lines folded inside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:209:"This one was signed for at neither. I shall come to that." Vastin undid the straps with his right hand, holding the leather down with the left, and folded the flap back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:295:They went down off the rise in file: a quiet man in front, and a girl with a case in her left hand, and a big one with a box, and a small one with a hide. A boy of nineteen came after them, who had been taken to a great many registries in his life, and sent for by some, and filed in all of them. Last came an old man on a stick, who had been a marshal and was learning to be nobody. Cael had never before walked toward such a place because he had chosen to. He kept his eyes on the lowest wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:Cael dropped back to him at the top of the rise. He had not looked forward to it. He was nineteen, and the man beside him had told people for forty years on which side of a line they stood; and Cael had now to tell him how to walk along a road.

