editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:23:> - **Ledger state.** It is the night before day 278; crew purse 118 marks; deployment thirty; ten fragments; Shadow still sealed after two prior uses; Anchor still two counted uses; Tide unused here; still place untouched (`manuscript/chapter-08.md:151–153`; `manuscript/chapter-09.md:263–265`; `manuscript/chapter-12.md:291–295`). Cael has not touched the page and has promised a daylight ruling at the plank with hands (`manuscript/chapter-12.md:223–241,267–299`).
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:7:*Inventory, because I do one at the start of anything, and a winter is a thing. I am nineteen. Ten confirmed. The still place: not a fragment, not the Quiet, read three times, and not touched since a healer said stop. Ninety-six in hand. The inn's six, for the back room where the sheets are, came out of the hundred and two before the snow, and is not coming out again. Nothing owed. Three hundred entered on Pike's sheet as taken and never to be paid, which is a kind of money too.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:23:*A hundred and sixteen in hand. Fence: paid. Pike wrote PAID and the five names under it and the line under that, and that was the tenth line under our names on his page, and he did not say tenth, because the season line above it is still blank, and he was not going to write a number under a blank he had left on purpose.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:33:Karis had not moved. Her pencil was on the notebook and had not touched it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:21:He read to the end of the line. Then to the end of the page. Then he put a scrap of leather in it and closed the thing and put both hands flat on it, which was, Cael saw, exactly what Karis did, and looked up, and looked at Karis, and at nothing else in the chamber.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:29:He did not touch them. He looked. He looked at the first set for as long as it takes a man to read a page he already knows, and then at the second, longer, and then at the third, and his hand came up off the table and went toward the third set and stopped, a hand's width off, and stayed there, and Cael saw it stay and had seen a hand do that before, on a table, near a page.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-09.md:15:"Fifty-two sheets." He turned round then. He looked at her — not at Cael, not at anyone else on the ring road; at her, at the flat under her arm, at her hands. "I eat at noon," Cleon said. "I read while I eat. You may sit across from me and not talk until I've finished the page I'm on, and then you may tell me where the floors are, and then I will decide whether I have been wasting thirty years or not. Those are the terms."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md:83:"I read the provision years ago," she said. "In a register, when I was reading everything. I never thought I'd stand at the counter it's kept at. I never thought I'd have a reason." She looked at the flat under her hand, and the page on it. "Four hands."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md:99:She went out the street door with the flat under her arm and the page on top and the notebook in her hand and nothing else, because a working researcher attaching to a registered practitioner carries her work and not her boxes, and she stopped at the door and looked back at the long room, at the four of them, and at Cael, who was sitting at the table with the log open and the pencil in his hand and his back to the wall, in the district where the unranked were allowed.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-06.md:11:He catalogued the camp because a camp at a fork was a thing he had done once already and had written down, and he wanted the second one honest. Seven of them and the mule, on the road's north verge where the ground was dry, with the fire small and the rise black against the west and the gap black against the north. Brom with the boot off again and the foot up, saying nothing about it. Lira on the far side of the fire with her two hands round a cup, using both. Karis with the flat under her arm even now, because she had stopped putting it down, and the citation page on top where she could reach it. Seln at the edge of the light, where the light stopped being useful, facing the road. Vastin on the road side, with the stick across his knees, a day less spent and a day more silent, watching a crew make a camp as a man watches a drill he has never seen and does not intend to interrupt. And Oryn, with her roll beside her and her hands flat on her knees and her face square to the fire, which was how she sat at everything, and which meant — he had learned this at a marsh — that she was about to say a finding.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-06.md:61:He did it in front of her, because that was the form. Hesk's satchel, the flap, the log in the pocket built for it, the plain hand. He opened it to the day's page — the two hundred and eightieth, the spring, the slab, the three readers, the sheet he had not written down — and under it, in front of the fire, in front of her, he wrote:
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-06.md:111:The four of them stood at the road's edge and watched, because it was a thing you watched. Brom with his weight on the good foot. Lira with her two hands at her sides, not in her coat. Karis with the flat under her arm and the page on top of it, looking at the north track as if it were a line on a map that she had not drawn and could not measure. Seln looking at the gap, where a road went that he had never walked and never would. And Vastin, a pace back, the fifth, looking at the four of them watching, because that was the thing he had not seen before, and he was a man who catalogued too, in his way, and had just found a thing that was not in his catalogue.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-06.md:121:He had read the name on a page in a square hand, and heard it in a healer's mouth, and in a researcher's, and in a marshal's, and had built, without meaning to, a city out of the four of them, and the city on the horizon was not that city; it was a bluff, first — a long grey bluff standing up out of the plain to the west with the river going round its foot — and then it was the walls, three of them, one above the other, stacked up the bluff's flank in tiers as a hold stacks its terraces, each wall a ring and each ring smaller than the one below it, so that all of it went up the hill like a thing that had been made to be looked at from below. Roofs inside the rings. Smoke. A road going up through the tiers by gates, and he could see the gates from here, three of them, one in each wall, and the road going through each and switching back to the next.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:93:A day he knew. A day he had a page for, in a log that was in Hesk's satchel on the ground by his boot, in the plain hand, the first entry in it that was not a schoolboy's, written at fourteen in a room with a door that locked from the outside. He did not open the satchel. He did not need the page. He had the day as he had the day he was born, which is a thing you do not look up.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:127:She put the rows back in it in their order, with the one page — the citation, a title and a place — on top, where she could reach it without opening anything, because she had decided at some point in the last hour that it was the page she would be reaching for, and she stood up from the slab with the case under her arm and the notebook in her hand and the road going west past the spring toward a rise that had, on the far side of it, a city she had never seen, and said what she had.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:65:The council took less than a sunset, as the one on a crest above the fourth had, and it was the same council with the answer the other way round, and he catalogued that, because a thing decided twice in two directions is a thing whose reasons should both be on the page. On the crest there had been five, and no healer within four days, and a circle twice the size of anything they had entered, and three sentences of reasons not to, and they had not. At the rim there were eleven, and a healer a stride off, and a circle two and a half times the Ring, and the same three sentences, and one more, which was that the line ended here and there was nothing past it to walk to instead. Nobody said the fourth sentence. Karis folded the map. Lira looked at him. That was the council, and it had decided what it had decided on a bridge and at a fire and over a map, which was that he was going in, alone, and that the eleven were going to stand at the line while he did, with their Paths gone at a stride and a healer's hands useless a stride in, because that was the only way it could be done and every one of them had known it since a landing in the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:62:10. **KEEP — The handover (277th evening)**, in Vastin's stated order. First, the records: a courier's flat of six years of survey abstracts, the anomaly column ("Total"), six rows, "More than four", and the completion report as the last sheet. Second, the page: "—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—". Third, the technician's sheet: the deep-layer line and the brush bar; Karis copies it and writes "not read". Seln goes still and does not say the sum. Vastin's vertigo; Karis owns the response. Cael's hand stays a hand's width from the page. Nobody says *west*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:87:35. **KEEP — The shale camp (305th).** The blanket. The rows lean along the bearing. The point is fixed. **The lamps finding** (§10). Vastin: "If a man designed the apparatus, then the sigils —" / the ruler rule. Two pages. Cael does not add his own third page (the Kindling date). **The directive and the objection**: "That's not a monolith. That's a crack." Seals unnamed. "The Compact … Yes. Part of it." The broad man's "Aye". The healers' third exchange (hands in the other's work). Seln on the pass. The fixed close (§10).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:215:- **The three papers.** (1) **The records:** a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather; six taped rows, a year to a row, of survey abstracts measured in lengths, knots and strides, with the dead surveyor's four-sign index printed at their heads; an **anomaly column** in which every sheet, in every hand, for six years carries the one word *Total* (an entry, not a sum; no figure); "More than four" (Karis, counting distinct sign-groups; she gives no number); the Fallow completion report is the last, loose sheet, and carries **five strokes for a name and a count of six** (locked Book 7 ch40, ch43), not five names. (2) **The page:** a COPY in Vastin's own hand of one line from an old register, carried in oilcloth in his coat: "—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—". "The Architect" is a title, not a name, and a word Karis has never met in any stratum. It is never called a leaf; **the leaf has not appeared**. (3) **The technician's sheet:** the two protected lines with a date between them, a brush bar across what followed, and Karis's *not read*. **The date is a Compact date, a registry year and a day in it, and is never printed as figures** (the source gives none; no owner decision is needed). The office had filed the sheet at the end of the second row and it lies there again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:219:- **Cael has not touched the page.** He will rule at the plank in the morning (the 278th), in daylight, and ask for hands. Nobody said *west* in the back room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:239:- **Vastin as he arrives (flag):** a drover's coat of brown frieze, too short in the sleeve, bought "at the last market inside the Line"; the grey one left on its hook; an ash stick cut out of a hedge; a pack with one strap mended in cord; a small purse on a cord; the flat carried under the pack against his back, and **a single copied page** inside his coat in oilcloth. **The knee** "had begun to complain at about the hundredth mile and had left off being civil at the two hundredth"; no side is given; ten miles a day. He left "the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it". At the last station he looks in at the plate and remembers only what Book 5 shows: a plate, a file and a set of questions carried up a bluff, "and all three had told him nothing"; and that he declined a second reading and never said why. He crossed the sill on the 265th and reached the western landing on the evening of the 276th. His left hand would not open after the strap and has not lain flat since. He has paid a copper for the fare, a copper and a half for a loaf, a copper for a bowl and a copper for the loft. He sleeps in **the loft over the tap, up the keeper's stair**.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:243:- **The page (repaired): a copy.** One sheet of the house's plain desk paper, folded twice, in Vastin's own square upright hand, in fresh ink. He copied the line "from a record that is older than the section it is filed in, in a room where I had the right to read and no right to copy", with the word and the place "exactly as they are written there, letter for letter", and the schedule of housings round it (a number, a date of setting and a mender's mark to each) to show where the line falls. He read the pages on both sides and did not copy them. **No cut edge, no blade, no inquiry, and no prompt from the ninth fragment.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:254:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: thirty** (the read on the ground at the landing, the 277th). Shadow used twice in all and shut; held at dusk on the 272nd and the 277th. Anchor's counted uses still two. Tide: Oryn twice, himself three times, nobody else; he did not ask to read the knee. **The still place** has not been read, touched or named. He has not touched the cord or either brass edge for a hundred and nine days; the ruling is *hold*. His near sight went at the landing and was back for print by the afternoon. **He has not ruled.** He will say it "at the plank in the morning, in daylight" and ask for hands. He has not touched the copied page; his hand lay a hand's width from it for the whole of a lamp. **He has not read the technician's date**: he was at the table's far end; he knows that it is a registry year and a day, and that it is the line at which Seln stopped; he chose not to go up the table and read it. He lost the bout to Lira, two touches to three. He has written to Ephram. He kept back his own question to Vastin (why the second reading was declined). He has told Vastin nothing of what he carries.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:266:**Vastin.** No office, no seal, no rank; he left the seal in a tray "some months" ago and walked three hundred miles. **No age is stated.** A bad knee; raw hands; a left hand that will not lie flat. **No Iron Wall has been shown or named.** He has handed over three things in his own order and holds Karis's receipt in his coat. He has said: that he holds no office and has no sanction; that the layer cannot be opened ("There is no key"), on the authority of the office's own page; that the one line which should name its maker cites a title and sends its reader to Treswick's lower archive (his own copy of it); that nothing of his follows him and the Stair's rim is not his; that the pending paper is unchanged; why he did not take it to a council. He knows the six by name. **He does not know** the mechanism, what Cael carries, the instruments' behaviour in Cael's hand, Karis's case or the sites (beyond hearing Brom say "four on the hide" and Lira name the Stair), who held the abstracts, which classification event the sheet means, or what is under the bar. He has not weighed the sheet's date. He has not read the flat's last row through, and nothing has been said of a directive or an objection. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. He was sent to bed, began to argue twice, took four stairs fast and the rest slowly; whether that was the order or the knee is not settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:272:**Objects and money.** **A hundred and eighteen marks in hand**, unchanged. The reserve sealed. Owed: **sixty yards of four-strand rope** to the ferryman (the cordage is on the western landing; his one hauling rope has two yarns gone). **The hurdle is paid.** **The letter to Ephram is sent.** The three boxes are at the hearth's end, roped. Stake thirty-two has still never been driven. On Karis's table: the flat (the sheet inside it), the broker's paper beside it, Seln's wand beside that, and the copied page at the far end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:280:**Open threads.** A ruling to be given at the plank in the morning. A page that Cael has not touched. A date that stopped Seln and that Cael has not read. A bar with something under it. A count that Karis has not checked. A paper that came to a desk with no routing note. A sentence that a healer has swallowed three times. A knee that nobody has asked about. A man in a loft with a receipt and nothing to do. A rope on the wrong bank. A captain's term with no day in it. A question that Cael kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:290:- **Ledger state.** It is the night before day 278; crew purse 118 marks; deployment thirty; ten fragments; Shadow still sealed after two prior uses; Anchor still two counted uses; Tide unused here; still place untouched (`manuscript/chapter-08.md:151–153`; `manuscript/chapter-09.md:263–265`; `manuscript/chapter-12.md:291–295`). Cael has not touched the page and has promised a daylight ruling at the plank with hands (`manuscript/chapter-12.md:223–241,267–299`).
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:153:He had thought the line was drawn in ink. He had thought that because ink was what he read it in. He stood at the mouth of the pass with the haze on the plain and the sound of a captain getting up off his knees behind him, and understood, for the first time, that the line ran through the pass as it ran through the sites, and that what it was drawn in was not ink and never had been, and that he was going to follow it to the end anyway, tomorrow, and that he was going to write that down tonight in the plain hand, and it was going to be the truest thing on the page, and he did not want it to be.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:5:Not the thing. The thing was on a leaf under a registry and in a notebook under Karis's arm, and it had been said once, aloud, in a hole, and was not going to be said again by anyone who had heard it, because a woman had asked that it be the record's and not theirs and the five of them had agreed to that without a word. He told Lira the only thing that was his to tell, which was that the thing she had heard read was on a page now, in Karis's hand, and that she should read the page before anybody talked at her about it, and she said, "Now?" and he said, "At the fire," and she went up the rest of the stair ahead of him with her two hands at her sides and did not look back, because Lira did not look back at a thing she was going to have to look at properly later.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:19:Cael took the notebook from Karis. She gave it to him without opening her eyes. He opened it to the page — the passage in the small square hand; the patch drawn as a patch, with its one upright stroke; the three words under it — and crossed the yard to Lira, at the brazier's far side, and put it in her hands, open, and did not say anything, and stepped back.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:27:She read it the way she did everything, which was fast, and then she read it again, which she did not do with anything, and her face did not change, because Lira's face did not change when a thing mattered; it changed when a thing did not. She read the three words at the bottom. She looked at the patch drawn as a patch. She closed the notebook, and held it a moment, and handed it back across the brazier, and he took it, and she looked at him — not the count; not a finding; the look she had given him in a hall at sixteen when a marshal had gone inside to read a page — and said the one true thing, which was not comfort, because Lira had never once in her life mistaken comfort for the truth.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:51:She said it with her eyes shut, from the bucket, with her hand on the notebook that Cael had given back to her: "Two pages. The leaf says a man made the apparatus and why. A dead surveyor says a hundred lamps go out on one wire. I'm not drawing a line. I've said it in a hole and I'll say it at a fire. I've a year and a week of not-guessing behind me and I'm not going to spend it tonight because I'm tired." She opened her eyes and looked at Cael, once, across the brazier. "And I'm not going to say the third word again. I said it once, to the leaf. It's yours now. Do what you want with it." And she shut her eyes, and was, he thought, asleep before the sentence had finished being in the air, sitting up, on a bucket, with her hand on a book, which was a thing he had seen her do at a marsh and had not seen since.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:51:"Every sheet," she said. "Every perimeter. Every practitioner the office ever sent to a line with a sigil, and every one of ours, and Teague's four at the Hall in the autumn, and the seven on the shelf yesterday who wouldn't step over a stake. It's the same. Not the same *kind* of thing — the *same*. The same stride. The same instant. A Silver goes at the line at the stride a Copper goes at. An Iron goes where a Tide goes. A man forty years on a Path goes where a boy a month on one goes, and neither of them goes a hair before the other, and neither of them a hair after, and it doesn't matter who they are, or what they've done, or how they were taught, or whether they believe in it." She put her good hand flat on the column. "I've got it on more than twenty sheets in an office's hand and on every page of my own since the Ring and on four floors of walls that say nothing about it at all, and I said it once at a spring and stopped, and I'm not stopping now, because a finding you stop three times is a finding you're afraid of, and I'm not afraid of it. I'm afraid of what comes after it, and that's a different thing."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:69:"I know where that sentence goes," she said, to him, not unkindly. "I've known since the leaf. You know where it goes. Everyone at this fire knows where it goes. There's a leaf that says a man made the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark, and set an apparatus over the land, and there's a column that says every mark goes out like a lamp when a wire's cut, and there's a word on both that's the same word, and the sentence that joins them is about six words long and I could say it in my sleep. I'm not going to." She opened the notebook, with the bound hand, and turned to a page, and wrote on it, and turned to another page, and wrote on that. "Two findings. Two pages. What the leaf says, which is a record and says what it says. What the column says, which is a measurement and says what it says. I've put them on separate pages and I'm not drawing a line between them, because a line's a thing you draw when you've measured both ends, and I've measured one end and read the other, and reading isn't measuring. I've spent a year at the foot of a stair learning the difference. I'll not throw it away at a fire because the sentence is easy."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:31:He had predicted it. He had written it down the previous night, in the plain hand, in the dark, and had thought, writing it, that it was the truest thing he had put on the page in a year, and he stood at the middle of the oldest quiet anyone had ever made, with the eyes coming back slowly and the floor coming back with them, grey, level, to a rim of stumps a thousand metres round with eleven people at it who were too small to count, and there was nothing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:5:Karis first, with the case under her arm and the copies inside it — the leaf's front and back, the gap drawn where the gap was, and the list of parts, which had taken all the day before and had been finished at the bell with Cleon's finger on the last line — and the ledger inside her coat, and the notebook in the bound left hand because it could hold that. Brom with the kit and the first box on the good shoulder and the other shoulder carried, still and slightly forward, in the set it had kept since a hall in the autumn, on a foot that had walked to a city and in one and out of it and had been kept dry, as a healer had said, and had stopped being a thing he mentioned. Lira with the third box and two arms to carry it. Seln at the back with the cache and his eyes on the gate behind them, counting who came out of it. Vastin with the stick, and the right knee bound under the trouser, and sixteen days of a month gone on it, and the weight going onto it a little more each morning than the morning before, which Oryn was not there to forbid. And himself, with the middle box, and the straightedge inside his coat and the dividers beside it, and twelve things held to nothing, and a page copied from a leaf that said what he was, folded flat against the log where the letters went.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:137:She said it to the sheets, with her bound hand flat on the first row and her good hand on the one page that had ridden on top of the case since the spring, the citation, a title and a place, and then she began, and he had all of it in the log by the end and could not afterward have said in what order she had said it, because she had said it in the order the sheets came, and the office had not known what it was filing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:75:*Twelve. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide; Iron Wall, from a plane in a cut; Rune, from a lamp. The still place at the middle of them, not read, not touched, not the Quiet, not a fragment. A cord that reads everyone but me. A row of sheets that counts one short in my hands. A straightedge and now the thing that goes with it.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:97:He had a page for it. That was all that he did with it. He had a page in the log for things the instruments did wrong around him and it had three entries on it — a still place a reading could not read; a cord that counted forty-one and a piece; a row of sheets that came out one short in his hands — and he added a fourth, standing, without taking the log out, in the part of him that wrote things down: a light on a hill that was not steady for the space of a step, once, for him. Deniable. Every one of the four was deniable. He had known since he was fourteen that he was the thing the instruments were not built for, and here was the biggest instrument on the continent, four tiers up, in the sun, doing the thing the small ones did, and he filed it where the small ones were filed, under the unreadability that had followed him across a continent, and let it stand, and did not cross.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:15:"Somebody's name," Karis said. She had followed his eye. "There was one. There's the first stroke of it. There's nothing else, and there's not going to be, because it's not faded; it's *gone*. Scraped. Whether a man did it with a knife the day after it was written or a thousand thumbs did it over the years going to that one place on the page to touch it, I can't tell and Cleon can't tell and nobody ever will." She put her bound hand flat on the table beside the leaf, not on it. "I've read the rest. I read it yesterday, with the board and the Hall's fourth sheet as the key, and again last night, and a third time this morning, and I'm going to read it aloud now, once, because that's how a finding's said, and then I'm not going to say it again, and none of you are going to say it back to me, because I want it to be the record's and not mine."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:37:"That's the passage," Karis said. "There's more on the leaf — a list of the measure's parts, the seat's, the tier's; I've the shape of it and not the words yet; it's the same instruction-make Cleon read on the floors, and it's older than the floors, and the floors were cut from it or from a thing like it. But that's the passage. That's what the citation on Vastin's page pointed at across a continent. That's the thing under the registry." She took her hand off the table. "I'll say what it says, once, in my own words, and then I've said it. It says the thing the Compact calls the Path system was *made*. By a person. The record's word for him is the one on Vastin's page — the Architect; it's a title, not a name; the name's gone. It says he made it about four hundred years ago, by the date at the leaf's head, which is a date I can read because the board taught me the numerals. It says he made it *for a reason*, and the reason's the only thing on the leaf the writer cared that anybody remember, and the reason is the un-bound." She looked at the word on the leaf, where it stood, in the antique hand, live, a thing somebody had written down as the name of a thing that walked in the land. "That's the word from the confiscated record. That's the word I've carried since a stratum. It's not retired here. It's not a limit. It's a *kind*. There were people the record calls that, before the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark, and the whole of the apparatus was set over the land so that there would not be any more of them."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:85:"Is a thing a record says a man made four hundred years ago," Karis said. She said it at once, and plainly, and not unkindly, and she said it looking at Vastin and not at the leaf, because it was a thing that had to be said to a face. "That's what the record says. It says he made a thing and set it over the land and it says why. It doesn't say what the thing he made has to do with yours, or with mine, or with the thing on a station's roof four tiers up, and I'm not going to say it either, because I've a leaf and a board and a floor, and a leaf and a board and a floor are facts about a leaf and a board and a floor." She put her hand flat on the notebook. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler. Not about the hand that held it. I've said that at a slab and a marsh and I'll say it in a hole. I've two findings this morning, and they go on two pages, and I'm not drawing a line between them, because I can't see the line and I'm not going to guess one."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:89:Cael catalogued the two pages, because he could see them from the corner: the notebook open, the bound hand flat on the left-hand page and the pencil on the right, and the left with the leaf's words on it in her small hand and the right with the column's four words and a date, and the gutter between them, a finger wide, with nothing in it. He had watched her not write in a gutter before, at a slab, with a wind lifting the sheets. He watched her not write in it now, in a hole with no wind, with a marshal across the table who had just asked her to, and understood that the not-writing was the same act in both places and had cost more in the second, and that she had paid it without looking up, and he entered the gutter as the thing the morning had found, and did not write in it either.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:99:She copied it into the field notebook, in her own hand — the small square hand that had copied fifty-two sheets of a wall and a kit's ledger and a marshal's six years — from the leaf, a run at a time, with the board beside her for the letters she was not sure of and Cleon's finger going above the leaf ahead of her pencil where a bearer was, and she did not copy the whole leaf; she copied the passage, because the passage was the finding and the rest was the work, and the work was for the road. She copied the scraped place as it was. She did not leave a gap on her page and she did not write a word in it; she drew the patch, the width of it, with its rough edge, and the one upright stroke at its left, and the nothing after, so that a reader of her page would see what a reader of the leaf saw, which was a name's length of a thing that had been taken back to the fibre, and would not be able to say, any more than she could, whether a knife had done it or a thousand thumbs.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:11:Karis sat down. She sat where she sat at a source — the near side, the lamp to her left, the notebook flat, the pencil in the hand that did not turn pages — and she put both hands on the table, and looked at the pack, and did not look at Vastin at all, which was, Cael thought, the greatest courtesy she had ever paid anyone.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:29:"Total," she said. "At the line. Every one. The same distance, the same instant." She turned a sheet. Another. "They wrote it the same way six years ago that they wrote it last summer. Nobody in six years has put a second word in that column." She wrote it then, in the notebook — the abbreviation, the six years, every hand — on the page she kept for observations she could not file, and Cael saw that it was the second entry on that page, and that the first had been a ring at Thornwater.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:71:She stopped. She read it again, to herself. Then she put the page back where he had set it, on the bare end of the table, apart, and took her hand off it, and Cael saw the hand come off the page as if the page had a heat in it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:83:Cael looked at the page on the bare end of the table. Four lines, in a square hand, folded twice, a word and a place. He did not know what he had expected a name to look like when it came. He had expected it, he found, to look like a name. This looked like a shelf-mark. It looked like the thing at the bottom of a file, under everything, that told you where the rest of the file was, and he had spent five years and a continent staying out of files, and here was the oldest one on the continent telling him where it was kept, and it was west, and inside.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:85:His hand was on the table. It was near the page. He noted the distance — a hand's width, a little less — and did not close it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:137:It was still on the table. It was still a hand's width from the page. He had not moved it in all the last thing and all the man's leaving, and Lira had watched it not move as she had watched it not move at a table at seventeen with a man in grey across from it, and she looked at it now, and then at him, and did not say anything, and did not need to.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:143:"No." He took the hand off the table and put it in his lap, and the page stayed where it was. "You didn't."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:149:The lamp burned down toward its working red and Karis did not trim it, because she had stopped seeing it. The six rows lay the length of the table with the frost creeping in at the window's edges behind them, and at the bare end, past the sixth row, apart from all of it, the one page lay folded open on its own — four lines, a title, a place, and nothing else — and Cael's hand was in his lap now and had been near the page for the length of a lamp and had not once touched it, and the six of them knew the distance to a hand's width and did not say that either.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:153:The lamp's light lay along the six rows and stopped at the page. He looked at the roads he could not see, east and west, and at the page he had not touched, and at the woman writing three questions in the order she had them, and did not write anything down himself, because it was not his to write yet, and a thing that was not yet his was a thing he could still refuse.

