editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"Five," she said. "I said more than four, by a lamp, and that I should give a number when I had checked it in daylight. It is five. It is the same five in every one of six years; I cannot read a sign of them, and I can tell one group from another as I can tell your five faces."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:39:Karis looked at the fifth group's sheet, which she had not lifted from its row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:41:"The fifth is larger again than the fourth. I have read its measure and nothing else, and I shall say nothing else until I have turned every sheet that it has. It is not on the hide. I do not know where it is. These are an office's signs for a place, and I cannot read them; if I could, I should not have needed to walk here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:55:"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the office's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:59:The measure did not change. That was the first thing, and she read it three times before she would say it. Each of the five places had been run out from its eight stations in every one of six years, by four different surveyors, to judge by the figures' shapes; and not one radius had altered by a single stride. She had expected that a thing would grow, or shrink. A marsh did, and a river-bed. These had sat where they were as a millstone sits.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:113:"There is no routing head on any sheet. That you knew. There is no surveyor's name; a field book is signed, and an abstract ought to carry the name that the book carried, and these carry a number in its place. There is nothing to say who ordered a man to walk round the same five places for six years. And there is no column for a cause." He let that stand a moment. "A form is a list of the questions that its maker expected to be asked. Somebody expected to be asked the day, and the measure, and whether the cord was true. Nobody expected to be asked why the thing happens. I have never before seen an office measure a matter so carefully and show so little wish to understand it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:177:"It has a square. A printer cut a block with a ruled place in it, and a heading over the place, for a clerk to fill. Nobody rules a place on a form for a thing that is always the same." She was speaking very fast now, and very small. "It is a class. It is the kind of thing that a certificate has, at its head, where it says what its bearer is. And I shall tell you one thing more, and then I shall stop, since I can see the edge of what I know. On the sheets of the fifth place, that square is not filled as ours are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:Nobody at the slab asked who had filled that square, or by what rule, since there was not a person there who could have answered. Cael looked at five small marks. He had been stood on a plate once, and something had been written at the head of a sheet about him, in a place that a printer had ruled for it. It appeared that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He put that where he put the rest, and added no verb to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:"There are five places," the old man said slowly. He was not speaking to anybody. "They have been measured for six years by an office that told no other office. They are classed, by a mark that the survey's own people carry on their iron. A surveyor who formed a view of them had the space for his view struck off the form, and is dead. And a return upon the deepest thing that the Compact owns has been laid among their sheets, for want of a shelf, with a brush drawn through it." His voice had quickened, and gone level. "This has to go up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:239:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on five sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.

