editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/review-editorial-prompt.md:3971:Eight hundred metres across (twice the Ring); the Compact's registry-stamped iron a pace apart, numbered (thirty-four on the crest's stakes), the stroke-and-bar beside the number, in the ground "years, not one winter" (undated). Inside: the ground drops into a SINK; at the centre an arched bridge of seamless grey stone on two piers over the sink, the crown taller than a man; carvings on the piers' inner faces, same make, copied. The bearing runs along the arch. A Compact WATCH-POST on the perimeter shelf (timber hut, fire, kit under a hide, a two-timber barricade; seven hooded, two crossbows, one low practitioner) routed there after the completion report; broke east along the perimeter road; not pursued; no faces. Nothing happened to Cael on the crown (as at every site).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-20.md:175:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r1/chapter-20.md:157:On top of the pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-56.md:113:They were on the brow of the low ground before noon. The four crossbows had lain where he left them, by a grey stone, and the frost had kept every print about it; and from between two bars of iron the trail went off south and east, as plain as a ditch.
