editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-54.md:67:Cael ran. He went along the ledge under the northern wall, away from the place, since a second weight there was the last thing that Brom wanted; and at the next break he got his hands on the stone and hauled. His right forearm had stopped a crossbow's stock five days before, and it told him so. He came over the top on his belly, and turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
