+68. `manuscript/chapter-18.md`
+   old: `He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year and a day in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has copied the seat's paper for fifteen years does not see a station's date without turning it, any more than a carter sees a load without knowing what it weighs. The turning had been done before he knew that he was doing it. A day had come out.`
+   new: `He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had copied a Compact year and a day exactly as they stood; and a man who has copied the seat's paper for fifteen years does not see such a date without laying it beside the one he remembers. The comparison had been made before he knew that he was making it. A day had matched.`
+
+69. `manuscript/chapter-18.md`
+   old: `A sum done in the head, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages for it to come out otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. He might have had the difference wrong; it was a long while since he had needed it. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were three places where he could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of the three.`
+   new: `A sum done in the head, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages for it to come out otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were places where he could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of them.`
