+3. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `"They don't come for a cart that keeps its feet. They come for a declaration. So we give them one: small, and short, and a long way from anybody. At the top of the cut, eighty strides up. One point of Ember on a twist of tow, on the ice. It's out in a breath. And whoever sets it doesn't stay there."`
+   new: `"They come for a declaration. So nobody gives them one unless they're meant to be loud. Brom stands on the south stone above the sink. Vastin is a stride behind him. Lira takes the south scree and Seln the north. I go twenty strides west, past the sink. Karis and Oryn keep the mule at the east end. We show them nothing until we know where all of them are."`
+
+4. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `"They go to where it was," said Lira.`
+   new: `"They go to the loudest thing," said Lira.`
+
+5. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `"They go to where it was, and it's gone, and nothing's moving. So they stand. That's her fourth line. Five hounds, or more, standing on a stride of glass at the top of a hill with their heads swinging, and us below them, as quiet as posts." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, twenty strides down from the mark, on the south edge, where there's a flat stone to stand on that isn't ice. Lira above him on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. When they come down to try us, they try Brom first. He's wrapped."`
+   new: `"Five hounds, or more, and nothing from us for them to hear." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, on the south edge above the sink, where there's a flat stone that isn't ice. Vastin a stride behind him. Lira above them on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. I take the road twenty strides west, past the sink. Karis and Oryn stay at the east end with the mule."`
+
+6. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `"And who is to be loud?" said Karis. "It is my fragment that you mean to use. I have set ten thousand such points, and I should not burn my hand."`
+   new: `"And if they do not move?" said Karis. "A pack that has learned this road may not do what the others did at the ford."`
+
+7. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `"You'd be eighty strides up a hill of ice with nobody near you, and you've never had to walk away from a thing that was coming. I've done little else since my Kindling." He did not let it sound like a joke. "It's a piece of yours that I'll be using, and it's mine to pay for. I'm the only one of us who can be loud once and then be nothing. That's the use of me."`
+   new: `"Then we learn it before anybody declares." He did not let it sound like a promise. "If somebody has to be loud, it'll be once, in a place we've chosen. Until then the use of me is to stand where they can see a body moving and give them nothing else."`
+
+8. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `"You'll have the foot of the cut," Cael said to Karis. "With the clock, and the mule, and the boxes. It's eighty strides from the mark and sixty from Brom, and it's the last place in that road that anything will come to."`
+   new: `"You'll have the east end of the cut," Cael said to Karis. "With the clock, and the mule, and the boxes. It is behind all of us, and out of the road."`
+
+9. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `At sixty strides there was a flat stone at the road's edge, and Brom stepped onto it and set his feet, with the rock held against his thigh. Vastin stopped behind him. Cael did not see where Lira went; there was a small dry rattle on the scree above, and then nothing. Seln had left them at the foot, and was already nowhere.`
+   new: `Above the sink there was a flat stone at the road's south edge, and Brom stepped onto it and set his feet, with the rock held against his thigh. Vastin stopped a stride behind him. Cael did not see where Lira went; there was a small dry rattle on the south scree above, and then nothing. Seln had crossed to the north scree and was already nowhere.`
+
+10. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `It was twenty strides, and they took him longer than the sixty had. At the eightieth the cut's head stood above him, black and wet, with the seep coming out under it in a skin over the stones. He stooped, and laid a twist of tarred tow on the ice, in the middle, and stepped back two paces onto the turf. His mouth was dry.`
+   new: `He went twenty strides west past the sink, keeping to the south edge with the ice at his left boot. He stopped there and turned to face east. Brom's stone stood behind the place where the north bank broke toward the sink; Vastin was a stride beyond it, and the others were where they had said they would be. His mouth was dry.`
+
+11. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `*Yes*, he told the fifth of the ten, for the first time in earnest since a stair.`
+   new: `Grey backs came over the north edge beside the sink, all at once and without a sound.`
+
+12. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `A seed of yellow stood on the tar. It grew for one breath to the size of a barley grain, and that was all he gave it. He shut Ember as he would have shut a hand. The heat that ought to have gone somewhere came home along his left arm instead, though he had been holding nothing, and the old weal across that palm woke and stung as though he had laid it on a kettle.`
+   new: `Five came first, grey and long, over the worn places. Three more followed from the strip under the bank that the lip had hidden. Eight. He counted them without moving his lips.`
+
+13. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `He turned his back on the mark, and walked.`
+   new: `He stopped moving.`
+
+14. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `Wind had his feet before the first stride was down. It leaned him at the foot of the cut with everything that it had, and he told it no, and found that his own two legs had something to offer in its place: a walk, a little bent, close under the scree, such as a girl on a bench had beaten into him with the two halves of a count. He did not run. A thing that ran on that road would be looked at.`
+   new: `Wind leaned at his feet and he told it no. He was already where the plan put him, twenty strides west of the sink, and the only task left was to be a body and not a declaration.`
+
+15. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `He heard them at the tenth stride. It was a sound like gravel poured from a cart, away up on his left, beyond the bank; and it did not come from three places. It came from the whole upper half of the cut together.`
+   new: `He heard claws on ice and the dry spill of plates under the north bank. The sound came from the sink and from the strip above it together.`
+
+16. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.`
+   new: `They came over the north bank beside the sink, where Lira had found the worn places, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were eight, grey and long, pouring onto the road with their heads level.`
+
+17. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `He was two strides from Brom's stone. He stopped, and stood, and counted eight.`
+   new: `He was twenty strides west of Brom's stone, past the sink. He stood, and counted eight.`
+
+18. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `They reached the tow, where there was a smear of soot on the ice and nothing else. He waited for them to stand.`
+   new: `Nothing in the cut had declared. He waited for them to choose among quiet bodies.`
+
+19. `manuscript/chapter-14.md`
+   old: `They did not so much as check. The first of them set its feet on the glass at the mark, and turned its head down the hill, toward the foot, where three carts lay; and all eight of them came on.`
+   new: `Every head turned toward the only body still exposed on the road, twenty strides west. All eight started toward him. Behind them, an ash stick struck the road once.`
+
+20. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `He understood it in the time that it took them to cover the first five strides, and it was far too late to be of use.`
+   new: `The sound from the road reached him through his boots before he understood what Vastin had done.`
+
+21. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `They had learned the road. Nothing that had ever declared at the top of that glass had stayed there. It had gone down the hill backward, with a man hanging on its tailboard and a Path blazing in him, and it had come to rest at the foot; and three times the grey things had run to the head of the cut, and found the sound already sixty strides below them. They did not go any longer to where a declaration was made. They went to where it ended.`
+   new: `They had learned the road, but the road had never given them this. A declaration rose behind them and did not move. It filled the cut from bank to bank. Eight grey heads turned from Cael in the same instant.`
+
+22. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `And at the foot today there were a small pale woman, and a healer, and a mule.`
+   new: `At the east end, behind every station, Karis and Oryn stood with the mule.`
+
+23. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `He had put them there himself. He had called it the last place in that road that anything would come to.`
+   new: `He had put them there himself, out of the road and behind the crew.`
+
+24. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `He had come two strides below Brom's stone by then, and he stood there, as he had told six other people to stand. Brom was a post above him, with the rock against his thigh. Something on the scree had gone as still as the scree itself. That was the doctrine, and it was sound. They would go by. They would arrive at the foot, where nothing was loud, and they would stand there and swing their heads. Then one of them would try the nearest quiet body with its teeth.`
+   new: `He stood twenty strides west of Brom's stone, past the sink, as he had told six other people to stand. Brom was a post at the south edge with the rock against his thigh; Vastin was a stride behind him; something on the south scree had gone as still as the scree itself. Then the road answered Vastin's stick, and every hound turned back toward the louder thing.`
+
+25. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `Karis knew how to be a post. So did Oryn. He was not certain what a mule knew.`
+   new: `Karis and Oryn were at the east end, with the mule behind them. They were not the reason the pack turned.`
+
+26. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `There was a brown coat at his elbow.`
+   new: `There was a brown coat a stride behind Brom.`
+
+27. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `Vastin had not looked at the hounds since the first of them came over the bank. He was looking down the cut. Cael, who was two feet from him, saw exactly where. A mule's long ears stood against the light, sixty strides off, beside a small figure with something bright in its hand, and a square one with its arms bare. Then the grey head turned, and he looked up the hill at what was coming. His face did not change at all. It was the face with which he had read a case through to its last sheet.`
+   new: `Vastin had looked once toward the east end, where the mule's long ears stood against the light beside Karis and Oryn. Then he looked at the eight hounds between Brom and Cael. From twenty strides west Cael saw the grey head turn and the stick come down. Vastin's face did not change. It was the face with which he had read a case through to its last sheet.`
+
+28. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `It was two feet from his face. He could have put out his hand and touched it, and he did not.`
+   new: `It stood two feet behind Brom's back. The big man could have put out his hand and touched it, and did not.`
+
+29. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `The first hound was three strides off and running.`
+   new: `The first hound checked three strides from Cael, wheeled on the ice, and ran back toward the wall.`
+
+30. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `That was the second cost, and it was arriving already. It was going out of him as heat goes out of a forge when the door is opened, steadily, with nothing kept back. Cael's own Arbiter had been dark since the day that it woke, and yet he felt it from two feet away. He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside Karis with nine points alight, and neither had been a tenth of this. Every hound in that cut could feel it from the far side of the hill. It was the loudest thing that had been done on that road in the memory of the den.`
+   new: `That was the second cost, and it was arriving already. It was going out of him as heat goes out of a forge when the door is opened, steadily, with nothing kept back. Cael's own Arbiter had been dark since the day that it woke, and yet he felt it from twenty strides away. He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside Karis with nine points alight, and neither had been a tenth of this. Every hound in that cut could feel it from the far side of the hill. It was the loudest thing that had been done on that road in the memory of the den.`
+
+31. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `It was no more than a length from Cael. It did not so much as turn its eye to him; he was a quiet body, and there was a loud one two feet beyond. It went by his knee like a grey wind and took the old man by the stiff leg, a little below the joint, and shut its teeth.`
+   new: `It came round the south end no more than a length from Brom. It did not so much as turn its eye to the quiet man with the rock; there was a loud one two feet beyond. It went by Brom like a grey wind and took Vastin by the stiff leg, a little below the joint, and shut its teeth.`
+
+32. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `He could have used any of them. The road's rule was broken already, by a better man than himself, for a better reason than he had ever had. No hound in that cut would have turned its head for one point of Ember more.`
+   new: `He could have used any of them. The road's rule was broken already, by a better man than himself, for a better reason than he had ever had. No hound in that cut would have turned its head for a lesser declaration.`
+
+33. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `They would not have turned. He could have made himself as loud as he pleased, here, at the wall's end, and it would have altered nothing whatever. The sound that they wanted was here already, and he would only have been adding to it. Seven hounds were trying to get round a wall. Not one of the seven had any wish to go through it. Why should it? Everything that it had come for was standing at the end.`
+   new: `They would not have turned for anything smaller. He could have made himself loud at the wall's end and altered nothing; the sound they wanted was already there. Seven hounds were trying to get round the wall toward the man who held it. Somebody else had to become the reason they crossed its middle.`
+
+34. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `It was behind him, on the north side. The low bank ran down the cut there, inside the plane, with its top crusted in loose plates; and ten strides off, where the bank stood highest, there was a place on it as far from Vastin's end of the wall as the cut allowed. A straight line drawn from the south scree to that place went through the plane a little north of its middle.`
+   new: `It was farther west, on the south scree. Ten strides beyond him the loose plates made a place where he could land, go still, and leave the pack a straight return to the middle of the wall.`
+
+35. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `There was a crack like a wet sheet shaken out, and the cut jumped. He had left the road and come to the bank, with the ten strides taken out of the middle. The plates slid under his boots, and held. Then the lock came down on him.`
+   new: `There was a crack like a wet sheet shaken out, and the cut jumped. He had left the road and come to the south scree farther west, with the ten strides taken out of the middle. The plates slid under his boots, and held. Then the lock came down on him.`
+
+36. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `There were six left. They came off the scree in one slide. The bank that Lira had loosened came with them, a grey river of plates, and it put them onto the runnel's glass already running, six of them, straight at him, down a hill.`
+   new: `There were six left. They came off the scree in one slide and ran west toward the place where his burst had ended. He went still. They passed the place by a stride before the stopped shout ceased to matter; then every head turned east again, toward the wall that had never stopped being loud, and the six came back over the runnel's glass at a run.`
+
+37. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `He stood behind another man's wall and could not so much as lift a hand. He had trusted his feet and his skin to a great many things in his life: to a count, to a read of a pace and a half, to his own judgment of a floor. He had never before trusted them to something held up by another person's knee, and a person whom he had known as an officer.`
+   new: `He stood on the scree and could not so much as lift a hand while the pack turned away from him. He had trusted his feet and his skin to a great many things in his life: to a count, to a read of a pace and a half, to his own judgment of a floor. He had never before trusted them to a line held by another person's knee, and a person whom he had known as an officer.`
+
+38. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `Cael stood on the road between them, with his knife in his hand and nothing on it. His left palm stung. He had spent one grain of fire and one burst, and neither had touched a hound.`
+   new: `Cael stood on the road between them, with his knife in his hand and nothing on it. His breath was gone and the ten frameworks knocked together in him. He had spent one burst, away, and it had touched no hound.`
+
+39. `manuscript/chapter-15.md`
+   old: `Each of the ten had come to him so. He had stood within reach of somebody who was using a Path in earnest, in a fight that the person meant. Afterward, or in the middle of it, there had been a new thing in him, asking its question. He had just stood two feet from the largest declaration that he had ever felt. He had put his whole skin behind it.`
+   new: `Each of the ten had come to him so. He had stood within reach of somebody who was using a Path in earnest, in a fight that the person meant. Afterward, or in the middle of it, there had been a new thing in him, asking its question. He had just watched the largest declaration he had ever felt from twenty strides west and put his whole skin inside the line it held.`
+
+40. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "clear because it's empty, not because it's safe" — the plan quotes this in part and does not pin its speaker; coordinator to supply the protected line and its tag here]]`
+   new: `"Three. They'll den somewhere else. Tell the factor the sink's clear, and tell him it's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe," Seln said.`
+
+41. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`
+   old: `"That a plane stood across a cut, from a line that cracked the ice. That it stood for as long as he did not move, and went when he let it. That eight struck it and none passed." Her pencil went on. "And I have added a sixth line to my five. *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* I had the three wrecks under my eyes from the foot for the better part of an hour, and I did not read them either."`
+   new: `"That a plane stood across a cut, from a line that cracked the ice. That it stood for as long as he did not move, and went when he let it. That eight struck it and none passed." Her pencil went on. "And I have added a sixth line to my five. *A den beside a road learns the road.* I had the three wrecks under my eyes for the better part of an hour, and I did not read what the ice had taught the pack."`
+
+42. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "You do now," — the plan gives this line without its speaker; coordinator to supply the tag]]`
+   new: `"You do now," Cael said.`
+
+43. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`
+   old: `*I planned it wrong. They do not go to where a thing is declared. On that ice they go to where it stops. I put Karis and Oryn and the mule at the foot and called it the safe end.*`
+   new: `*I planned it without a wall. The den knew the road and the ice. When the wall went up they turned to the loudest thing, and the man holding it was the only reason they had to go round. I was twenty strides west, past the sink. The burst made me the reason long enough to turn them back.*`
+
+44. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`
+   old: `*The point: Ember, one grain, at the mark. In earnest. The left palm, though I held nothing.*`
+   new: `*Ember stayed shut. No point was spent at the cut.*`
+
+45. `manuscript/chapter-16.md`
+   old: `*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*`
+   new: `*Deployment since the Line: thirty-one.*`
+
+46. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"Five," she said. "I said more than four, by a lamp, and that I should give a number when I had checked it in daylight. It is five. It is the same five in every one of six years; I cannot read a sign of them, and I can tell one group from another as I can tell your five faces."`
+   new: `"More than four," she said. "I said that much by a lamp, and daylight has not made the signs readable. The same groups recur in every one of six years; I can tell one group from another, and I shall not call each group a place until the paper lets me."`
+
+47. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"Four on the hide," said Brom. "And one over."`
+   new: `"Four on the hide," said Brom. "Can you find those?"`
+
+48. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"And the fifth?" said Lira.`
+   new: `"And the rest?" said Lira.`
+
+49. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `Karis looked at the fifth group's sheet, which she had not lifted from its row.`
+   new: `Karis looked at the other sign groups, none of which she had matched to the hide.`
+
+50. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"The fifth is larger again than the fourth. I have read its measure and nothing else, and I shall say nothing else until I have turned every sheet that it has. It is not on the hide. I do not know where it is. These are an office's signs for a place, and I cannot read them; if I could, I should not have needed to walk here."`
+   new: `"I cannot match another group to the hide, and I shall not turn an unread sign into a fifth place. These are an office's signs, and I cannot read them; if I could, I should not have needed to walk here."`
+
+51. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `The measure did not change. That was the first thing, and she read it three times before she would say it. Each of the five places had been run out from its eight stations in every one of six years, by four different surveyors, to judge by the figures' shapes; and not one radius had altered by a single stride. She had expected that a thing would grow, or shrink. A marsh did, and a river-bed. These had sat where they were as a millstone sits.`
+   new: `The measure did not change. That was the first thing, and she read it three times before she would say it. Each of the four matched places had been run out from its eight stations in every one of six years, by four different surveyors, to judge by the figures' shapes; and not one radius had altered by a single stride. She had expected that a thing would grow, or shrink. A marsh did, and a river-bed. These had sat where they were as a millstone sits.`
+
+52. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"It is his," she said. "I should not swear to a hand on four words. I shall say that I see no difference." She read it then, to the whole slab, in the voice that she kept for another person's finding.`
+   new: `"The date says that the hand is dead," she said. "I should not swear to more on four words." She read it then, to the whole slab, in the voice that she kept for another person's finding.`
+
+53. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write." Her finger lay beside the ruled space, not on it. "He was a man who lived among instruments. He knew how a made thing behaves: it does the same at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule. That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."`
+   new: `"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write." Her finger lay beside the ruled space, not on it. "That is all the remark tells us. I shall not give his four words a fifth."`
+
+54. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"He does not say. I suppose that nobody could tell him who had made such a thing, or how it would be made; and a surveyor is not paid to suppose." She had taken her hand away. "I felt it, at three of those lines. So did four of you. There was no dimming. It went out at one stride, for a third rank and a first and a Copper alike, and came back at the same stride, whole. It was not as a person tires. It was as a row of lamps—"`
+   new: `"He does not say." She had taken her hand away. "I felt it, at three of those lines. So did four of you. There was no dimming. It went out at one stride, for a third rank and a first and a Copper alike, and came back at the same stride, whole. It was not as a person tires. It was as a row of lamps—"`
+
+55. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"There is no routing head on any sheet. That you knew. There is no surveyor's name; a field book is signed, and an abstract ought to carry the name that the book carried, and these carry a number in its place. There is nothing to say who ordered a man to walk round the same five places for six years. And there is no column for a cause." He let that stand a moment. "A form is a list of the questions that its maker expected to be asked. Somebody expected to be asked the day, and the measure, and whether the cord was true. Nobody expected to be asked why the thing happens. I have never before seen an office measure a matter so carefully and show so little wish to understand it."`
+   new: `"There is no routing head on any sheet. That you knew. There is no surveyor's name; a field book is signed, and an abstract ought to carry the name that the book carried, and these carry a number in its place. There is nothing to say who ordered a man to walk round the same four matched places for six years. And there is no column for a cause." He let that stand a moment. "A form is a list of the questions that its maker expected to be asked. Somebody expected to be asked the day, and the measure, and whether the cord was true. Nobody expected to be asked why the thing happens. I have never before seen an office measure a matter so carefully and show so little wish to understand it."`
+
+56. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"And the man who wrote them went down forty steps with a stake," said Brom slowly, "and never came up."`
+   new: `"And somebody took the space away the next year," said Brom slowly.`
+
+57. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the office's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."`
+   new: `"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the Compact date. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."`
+
+58. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"You wished to know what the form is. It is a survey abstract of the second schedule: that is to say, it is the sheet that stands in place of the field book when the book has been called in. The book itself does not survive it. I think that you hold the only one that ever escaped; and it escaped because its owner did not come home." His left hand, which would not open, lay on the stick's head. "The sheets are drawn at the year's end, in the office that has the charge of the stations. You will have seen that the days run strangely."`
+   new: `"You wished to know what the form is. It is a survey abstract of the second schedule: that is to say, it is the sheet that stands in place of the field book when the book has been called in. The book itself does not survive it. I think that you hold the only field book that escaped." His left hand, which would not open, lay on the stick's head. "The abstracts are drawn at the year's end, in the office that has charge of the stations. Their first column carries a Compact date."`
+
+59. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"I have seen that I cannot make them agree with the broker's paper, which gives a day past the Line."`
+   new: `"Then the date needs no private calendar to be read beside another Compact paper."`
+
+60. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"You will not. That office does not keep the seat's year; it keeps its own, which opens later. It is an old quarrel about who was founded first, and I shall not weary you with it. A day that is written in the stations' count must be turned before it can be laid beside a registry date, and the turning is a matter of taking off the difference." He gave the difference, which was a number of days that any child could have subtracted, and Karis wrote it in her margin. "Every junior does that sum in his first week, and gets it wrong once. A return that comes up from a station is dated so. A paper that goes down from the seat, to a district or to a person, is dated the other way."`
+   new: `"It does not," said Vastin. "The date is in the Compact's registry count. A return from a station and a paper from the seat can be laid beside one another without a conversion."`
+
+61. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of an iron stake that lay in a tarred case, three feet from her elbow, with its point still bright. It was on every bar of a fence that he had counted in the dusk. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.`
+   new: `Cael knew it before she had moved the pencil. It was struck into the head of stake thirty-two, which lay in a tarred case three feet from her elbow with its point still bright. He had seen it once more in the field, on stake thirty-four at the fourth site's line. A dead man had set it under four signs in his own book, where nothing obliged him to.`
+
+62. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."`
+   new: `"I found that on two of our places in the field: stake thirty-two at the Stair and stake thirty-four at the fourth. Stakes thirty-one and thirty-three do not carry it. And here it is on all four sites' sheets." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The office has never once left it out."`
+
+63. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"It has a square. A printer cut a block with a ruled place in it, and a heading over the place, for a clerk to fill. Nobody rules a place on a form for a thing that is always the same." She was speaking very fast now, and very small. "It is a class. It is the kind of thing that a certificate has, at its head, where it says what its bearer is. And I shall tell you one thing more, and then I shall stop, since I can see the edge of what I know. On the sheets of the fifth place, that square is not filled as ours are."`
+   new: `"It has a square. A printer cut a block with a ruled place in it, and a heading over the place, for a clerk to fill. Nobody rules a place on a form for a thing that is always the same." She was speaking very fast now, and very small. "It is a class. It is the kind of thing that a certificate has, at its head, where it says what its bearer is. That is the edge of what I know, and I shall stop there."`
+
+64. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"How is it filled?"`
+   new: `"Who filled it?"`
+
+65. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `"I shall draw it, exactly, and show it to nobody until I can say what I am showing. I have learned what comes of describing a mark before I can read it. I had two with and two without for the whole of a cold term, and I made nothing of them."`
+   new: `"I do not know. I know only that the same stroke and bar stands on all four sheets, drawn no larger on the place whose measure is double. I shall not say who set it there or why."`
+
+66. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "Two copies of one ignorance." — the plan gives this line without its speaker; coordinator to supply the tag here]]`
+   new: `"Two copies of one ignorance," Karis said.`
+
+67. `manuscript/chapter-17.md`
+   old: `Nobody at the slab asked who had filled that square, or by what rule, since there was not a person there who could have answered. Cael looked at five small marks. He had been stood on a plate once, and something had been written at the head of a sheet about him, in a place that a printer had ruled for it. It appeared that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He put that where he put the rest, and added no verb to it.`
+   new: `Nobody at the slab asked who had filled that square, or by what rule, since there was not a person there who could have answered. Cael looked at four small marks. He had been stood on a plate once, and something had been written at the head of a sheet about him, in a place that a printer had ruled for it. It appeared that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He put that where he put the rest, and added no verb to it.`
